//! The SOS call numbers — KERNEL-INTERNAL (design 140; sos/spec.md §5.7).
//!
//! Every op number, rights bit, status tag and handle type in the system, in
//! one place.
//! Exactly two things import this: the kernel's dispatch tables
//! (`sos/kernel/core/`) and the `sos` wrapper module the kernel package exports
//! to userspace (`sos/kernel/sysapi/`). Nothing else, ever.
//!
//! WHY IT IS SHARED RATHER THAN DUPLICATED: the dispatch and the wrappers are
//! the two halves of one contract. Held apart, they skew — and a syscall-number
//! skew is not a compile error anywhere, it is a working call that does the
//! wrong thing. Held together, renumbering is a one-line edit that either
//! rebuilds both halves or fails.
//!
//! WHY IT IS KERNEL-INTERNAL: the Zircon-vDSO discipline (ratified Aug 5).
//! NUMBERS ARE NOT ABI. Userspace links the exported `sos` module and calls
//! methods; it never writes `7` for an op, so the kernel may renumber freely
//! between builds. The one thing a process must agree with the kernel about is
//! the CALLING CONVENTION (§5.7: handle in a0, op in a7), and that lives in the
//! per-arch HAL stub, not here.
//!
//! WHY THESE ARE ENUMS AND NOT STATICS (design 145/153). Every family here is a
//! CLOSED NAMED SET of values, which is what an Int-inheriting enum is for: it
//! names the set, PINS the numbers so a reorder cannot renumber them, gives the
//! dispatch an exhaustive `match`, and turns the read at the trap boundary into
//! `from(raw:)` — a total function returning `E?`, so an unrecognized number is
//! DATA the kernel rejects rather than an index it trusts. The parallel statics
//! this replaced (`OP_SYSTEM_DEBUG_PRINT`, `SYS_ERR_BAD_OP`, …) could be
//! compared against anything and validated only by a hand-written `> MAX` test.
//!
//! HOW A FAMILY IS NAMED (ruled Aug 15, user). An op table or a rights set is
//! named for the spec §2 OBJECT it belongs to, spelled OUT, plus the
//! term-of-art suffix §5.7 already uses: `SystemOp`, `ProcessOp`, `ThreadOp`,
//! `EventOp`, `WaiterOp`, `SystemRight`, `ProcessRight`, `ThreadRight`,
//! `EventRight`, `WaiterRight`. No abbreviated object prefix — not `SysOp`, not
//! `ProcRight`, not `EvtRight` — because the abbreviation saves four characters
//! in a file nobody types into and costs every reader the question of whether
//! `Sys` means the System object or the syscall. The rule extends to anything
//! else here named after an object (`ProcessStatusKind`, `process_status_word`,
//! `EventMode`, `WaiterReadiness`), and the next object kind inherits it: name
//! the family after the row in the §2 table.
//!
//! HOW AN OP IS NAMED (ruled Aug 17, user). Inside a family an op reads
//! `object_operation`: the OBJECT KIND the op concerns leads, then the verb —
//! `ProcessOp.ThreadCreate`, `SystemOp.ClockGet`, `ClockOp.TimerCreate` — and
//! the typed method and the C export mirror it (`process.thread_create`,
//! `sos_system_clock_get`). An op whose RECEIVER IS the object it concerns keeps
//! the bare verb, there being no second noun to lead with: `Arm`, `Ack`, `Wait`,
//! `Signal`, `Join`, `Start`, `Exit`, `Yield`, `Now`, `Add`, `Remove`,
//! `Shutdown`, `DebugPrint`, `GetStatus`. What the rule buys is that every op
//! concerning a kind sorts together — a reader looking for what makes a Timer
//! finds `TimerCreate` without having to know which object is the factory — and
//! that a rights bit named after an op (`ProcessRight.ThreadCreate`) reads
//! exactly like the op it gates. A right naming a CAPABILITY rather than an op
//! (`Transfer`, `Mint`, `Read`, `Control`, `Attach`, `Debug`) is not under this
//! rule and keeps its own noun.
//!
//! Delivery in v1 is static linking through the manifest/module-path
//! mechanism. A genuinely mapped vDSO is an object-model-era upgrade that
//! changes none of the above.

// =============================================================================
// Object types
// =============================================================================

/// What a handle-table entry names.
///
/// `Unbound` is the reserved zero: handle 0 names nothing, so a zeroed register
/// can never be mistaken for authority, and a table slot that was never filled
/// reads as "no object" rather than as object type 0.
///
/// A `UInt8` backing because this is a kernel-internal field in `HandleEntry`,
/// never a number that crosses the trap boundary.
public enum ObjType: UInt8 {
    case Unbound = 0,
    case System = 1,
    case Process = 2,
    case Thread = 3,
    case Event = 4,
    case Waiter = 5,
    case Interrupt = 6,
    case Clock = 7,
    case Timer = 8,
    /// RAM AUTHORITY — a range of physical memory, derived by `Split` and
    /// installed by `Map` (spec §2.5; sawos design 2 D-2, grown by design 6).
    ///
    /// **UNIT 2 BUILT THE FIRST SLICE AND UNIT 4 BUILT THE REST, WITHOUT
    /// MOVING THIS ROW** — which is what the slice was shaped to make true.
    /// M3 unit 2's Memory was `{base, len}` and NOTHING ELSE: no pools, no
    /// derivation, no `map()`, no attributes and NO OP TABLE, minted only at
    /// boot from the build-emitted region table and NAMED as the two arguments
    /// of `process_create`. Design 6 gave it two ops, two rights and a slab a
    /// process spends THROUGH, and the number, the handle type and every
    /// existing caller stayed exactly as they were.
    ///
    /// §2.5 was never contradicted, only STARTED: unit 2's boot regions are
    /// that section's "pool roots given to the root server at boot", and unit
    /// 4 added the pools, the derivation and the mapping on top. What it did
    /// NOT add is a bounds reader — a region is still a CAPABILITY rather than
    /// a description, which is the one property of the slice that was load
    /// -bearing rather than provisional.
    case Memory = 9,
    /// DEVICE AUTHORITY — a range of memory-mapped registers (spec §2.5;
    /// sawos design 6 D-1).
    ///
    /// **A DISTINCT KIND, NOT A FLAG ON `Memory`**, and the reason is that
    /// every question about it answers differently. §2.5's pool ATTRIBUTE is
    /// design 46's intent marker, and making it a KIND is what turns "a Device
    /// MemoryObject can only produce Device mappings" from a rule somebody
    /// checks into a fact of the type system: an IoMemory's `Map` installs a
    /// device-attribute row and there is no other op on it that could install
    /// anything else, so a driver cannot get a cacheable view of a register
    /// block BY CONSTRUCTION.
    ///
    /// The lifecycle differs everywhere too, which is the other half: it is
    /// PINNED (never freed — MMIO is not memory that can be reclaimed), it is
    /// carved NON-EXCLUSIVELY (§2.5: "a fixed region may be handed out many
    /// times", so several drivers may hold windows onto one peripheral), and
    /// it has no contents — an image never carries bytes for it.
    ///
    /// ONE DISPATCH ARM IS THE ENTIRE COST, and what it retires is every
    /// flag-check the M2 device-grant placeholder scattered (`check_device_grant`
    /// in `kcore.loader`, `SegFlag.Device` in `imgformat`).
    case IoMemory = 10,
    /// ONE INSTALLED PROTECTION ROW, with its own handle (spec §2.5; sawos
    /// design 6 D-1).
    ///
    /// **A MAPPING IS AN INSTALLED GRANT ROW.** That is the whole of it on a
    /// no-translation machine (spec §5.5: an address is the same number in
    /// every process), so this object records WHICH process's domain carries
    /// the row and WHICH row it is — not a virtual placement, because there is
    /// no virtual to place at.
    ///
    /// **ONLY A MAPPING UNMAPS** (§2.5), which is what the object is for.
    /// Releasing a Mapping HANDLE frees the table entry and never the row —
    /// the entries-never-touch-objects rule (design 3 D-2), which is what
    /// makes §2.5's "dropped without unmap = permanent, safe-but-leaked" fall
    /// out of existing doctrine rather than being new law.
    case Mapping = 11,
    /// THE CLIENT END OF A CONNECTION — requests flow IN here (spec §2.1;
    /// sawos design 10 D-1 / ruling 2, built by design 13).
    ///
    /// **TWO KINDS, ONE SLAB, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF D-1's "ROLES ARE
    /// OBJECTS".** A Pipe carries traffic both ways, so "how many handles can
    /// still send" cannot be asked of one symmetric object — a parked receiver
    /// is itself a holder, which is the socket-`shutdown()` hole — and a
    /// rights-partitioned count on one object would be a SECOND LEDGER against
    /// design 7's one-ledger doctrine. So the bidirectionality DISSOLVES: the
    /// connection is a pair of counted ENDPOINT kinds, and "the writers went to
    /// zero" is "the client end reached zero references", which is the existing
    /// ledger with no new vocabulary.
    ///
    /// Both kinds TARGET THE SAME `PIPES` slot, and which of the connection's
    /// two reference columns a handle counts on is decided by its KIND — which
    /// §3's order establishes before anything else is asked. `kcore.refs` is
    /// where that becomes mechanical.
    ///
    /// **A SIDE REACHING ZERO RAISES PEER-GONE ON THE OTHER**, and the
    /// connection frees only when BOTH columns are zero. In unit 1 nothing
    /// parks, so peer-gone is visible as `SosStatus.PeerClosed` answers alone;
    /// unit 3 gives it a waitable level.
    case PipeInlet = 12,
    /// THE SERVER END OF A CONNECTION — requests come OUT here (spec §2.1;
    /// sawos design 10 D-1 / ruling 2, built by design 13).
    ///
    /// The mirror of `PipeInlet` in every respect except which column it counts
    /// on and which op it carries: an inlet posts, an outlet takes. Rights stay
    /// named for ops (the Aug-29 doctrine), so the ROLE is structural rather
    /// than a rights mask somebody counts by — which is exactly what let D-1
    /// answer the writer-count question with the ledger it already had.
    case PipeOutlet = 13,
    /// THE CLIENT'S CLAIM ON ONE REPLY — single-use, transferable (spec §2.1's
    /// ratified `PipeReplyHandle`; sawos design 14 D-1).
    ///
    /// **IT IS RING-SLOT STATE, NOT A THIRD SLAB.** A one-shot pair exists for
    /// exactly as long as one in-flight message does, and design 10 ruling 4's
    /// rider already pins the reply body to the staging slot that carried the
    /// request — so the claim lives WITH THE SLOT. `entry.target` for this kind
    /// is an EXCHANGE index (`connection * PIPE_INFLIGHT + ring slot`), which is
    /// the same flat arithmetic the bodies and lengths already use, one level
    /// down from `PipeInlet`'s "two kinds, one slab".
    ///
    /// **TWO COLUMNS AGAIN, AND THEY ARE THE ABANDONMENT STATE** (D-2, design
    /// 13's own property one level down): `PIPE_REPLY_REFS` counts the claims and
    /// `PIPE_REQUEST_REFS` counts the obligations, each column only ever falls,
    /// and which one a handle counts on is its KIND. The client dropping its
    /// claim IS abandoned-by-client; the server dropping its request without
    /// replying IS abandoned-by-server. No flag beside them says so.
    ///
    /// SINGLE-USE IS A PROPERTY OF THE EXCHANGE, NOT OF THE HANDLE: `Resolve`
    /// consumes the caller's ENTRY, and the exchange's state is what makes a
    /// second resolve impossible — one reply may be written and one resolve may
    /// succeed however many siblings a `Mint` produced.
    case PipeReply = 14,
    /// THE SERVER'S OBLIGATION TO SEND ONE REPLY — single-use, transferable
    /// (spec §2.1's ratified `PipeRequestHandle`; sawos design 14 D-1).
    ///
    /// The mirror of `PipeReply` in every respect except which column it counts
    /// on and which op it carries: a claim resolves, an obligation replies. It is
    /// minted at `PipeOutletOp.Take` into the TAKER's table, which is what makes
    /// it the delegation primitive — §2.1's canonical zero-copy example is a
    /// server GIVING this handle to a third party who replies straight to the
    /// original client, and `Transfer` is minted for exactly that.
    case PipeRequest = 15,
}

// WHICH KINDS ARE WAITABLE is deliberately NOT recorded here (§2.2). It cannot
// be a property of the kind alone: attaching a handle to a Waiter also spends a
// RIGHT, and which enum's bits that right lives in is decided by the kind — so
// the two questions are one question, answered in one exhaustive `match` in the
// kernel's `waitable_slot`. A `Bool` here would be the same fact in a second
// place, and the place that could go stale.

// =============================================================================
// Handles (spec §3)
// =============================================================================

/// A handle naming the kernel's System object.
///
/// Each object kind gets its OWN handle type (ratified Aug 7). Saw's distinct
/// `type` alias gives exactly the asymmetry a handle wants for free: it flows
/// TO `UInt` implicitly, so the syscall stub takes it with no cast and no
/// conversion is emitted — but a raw word, or another kind's handle, cannot
/// flow IN. Crossing into a handle type is an explicit construction, and there
/// are only two places that do it: userspace adopting its boot handle, and the
/// kernel's dispatch AFTER the table has resolved the handle to a System
/// object. That is what makes the type mean "validated as System" rather than
/// merely "a number".
///
/// The typing stops at the ABI boundary. The `@export`ed vDSO symbols and the
/// `sos_syscallN` stubs keep raw `UInt` words, because a C caller sees words
/// and the export whitelist is primitives; the handle TABLE indexes by word too.
///
/// This is TIER ONE of two — kind safety. TIER TWO, the OWNING tier, IS BUILT
/// (sawos design 3, M3 unit 2.75): every `kernel/sysapi` wrapper is a `NoCopy`
/// structs over these aliases whose `deinit` releases the word, so DROP IS
/// RELEASE and §3's no-DUPLICATE rule has its exact `NoCopy` correspondence.
/// The alias became the payload and nothing written against it changed, which
/// is what the additive claim promised.
///
/// **WHAT A HANDLE WORD IS MADE OF (sawos design 3 D-1).** The word is
/// `(generation << HANDLE_INDEX_BITS) | (index + 1)` on BOTH profiles — the low
/// `HANDLE_INDEX_BITS` bits are a 1-based table index, the rest of the portable
/// 32-bit budget is the slot's generation, and the top 32 bits of an arm64 word
/// stay zero. Same split and same width everywhere means the wrap behaves
/// identically on both machines: one story, one test. **THE SPLIT IS ONE NAMED
/// CONSTANT** (`HANDLE_INDEX_BITS`, default 8, below) with every mask and shift
/// derived from it, so moving it is a one-line edit.
///
///   - **THE INDEX IS 1-BASED, which is what keeps generation 0 free.** Slot 0
///     is usable and `NO_HANDLE = 0` still names nothing, because "names
///     nothing" is the INDEX FIELD being zero rather than the whole word being
///     zero. A malformed word like `0x100` — generation 1 over a zero index, at
///     the default split — is not equal to `NO_HANDLE` and must still name
///     nothing; the kernel's `lookup_handle` tests the field for exactly that
///     reason.
///   - **GENERATION 0 STAMPS NOTHING.** A slot's first-life word EQUALS the
///     bare index, so every contract written before generations existed
///     survives verbatim: §12's boot order (1 = System, 2 = Process, 3 =
///     Thread) that the hand-assembled payloads encode, and root's "boot
///     handle 1". Only a REUSED slot's word grows high bits, and no reuse
///     exists until a release happens.
///   - **IT IS BEST-EFFORT DETECTION OF A BUG, not a uniqueness guarantee.** A
///     word held across exactly `HANDLE_GENERATION_MODULUS` reuses of one slot
///     aliases silently.
///     Tables are per-process, so a wrap collision crosses no process boundary
///     and grants nothing; nobody later "fixes" this into unbounded
///     bookkeeping.
public type SystemHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming a Process object (§2 table). Same discipline as
/// `SystemHandle`: it flows TO `UInt` at the syscall stub and nothing flows in.
public type ProcessHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming a Thread object (§2 table).
public type ThreadHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming an Event object (§2.4). Same discipline again.
public type EventHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming a Waiter object (§2.2).
public type WaiterHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming an Interrupt object (§2 table, §9) — an IRQ line bound to a
/// waitable. Same discipline as every other handle type.
public type InterruptHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming a Clock object (§2 table; design 232 unit 1) — a granted
/// time source. Same discipline as every other handle type.
public type ClockHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming a Timer object (§2 table; design 232 unit 1) — a deadline
/// bound to the Clock that created it. Same discipline again.
public type TimerHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming a Memory object (spec §2.5; sawos design 2 D-2) — a sealed
/// range of physical memory. Same discipline again.
public type MemoryHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming an IoMemory object (spec §2.5; sawos design 6 D-1) — a
/// range of device registers. Same discipline again.
public type IoMemoryHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming a Mapping object (spec §2.5; sawos design 6 D-1) — one
/// installed protection row. Same discipline again.
public type MappingHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming a PipeInlet (spec §2.1; sawos design 13) — the CLIENT end of
/// a connection. Same discipline again.
public type PipeInletHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming a PipeOutlet (spec §2.1; sawos design 13) — the SERVER end
/// of a connection. Same discipline again.
///
/// Two handle types for two kinds, exactly as the §2 table's other rows get
/// one each: a word validated as an inlet cannot flow into a place that wants
/// an outlet, which is the whole reason each kind gets its own alias rather
/// than a shared `PipeHandle` with a role field.
public type PipeOutletHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming a PipeReply (spec §2.1; sawos design 14) — the CLIENT's claim
/// on one reply. Same discipline again.
///
/// **THE TYPED WRAPPER OVER IT IS `sos.pipe`'s `PipeReply`**, which is where
/// §2.1's ratified name lands: the tree renders a §2 object name at the typed
/// tier the way it renders `MemoryObject` as `Memory`, so the spec's
/// `PipeReplyHandle` is this alias and the owning wrapper is the short name.
public type PipeReplyHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming a PipeRequest (spec §2.1; sawos design 14) — the SERVER's
/// obligation to send one reply. Same discipline again.
public type PipeRequestHandle = UInt

/// A handle word the table can never hand out. Handle 0 names nothing, so a
/// zeroed register is never authority (see `ObjType.Unbound`).
///
/// Under design 3's split word this is the LOW BYTE's zero, at every
/// generation: `0x100` is as unbindable as `0` is, because the index a word
/// carries is 1-based and zero is not an index. The kernel's `lookup_handle`
/// tests the byte rather than the word, and the wrappers' disarm sentinel is
/// this value for the same reason — an armed wrapper's word always has a
/// non-zero low byte.
public static NO_HANDLE: UInt = 0

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE HANDLE WORD'S ENCODING — one constant, everything else derived
// (sawos design 3 D-1, as amended by the Aug-29 user note)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **THE SPLIT IS POLICY, STATED ONCE.** Where the index field ends and the
// generation field begins is a design choice this system will want to revisit —
// ~254 per-process handles is generous for a microkernel with sixteen and not
// obviously enough for one that grows — so it is ONE named constant with every
// mask, shift, bound and assert derived from it. Raising `HANDLE_INDEX_BITS` is
// a one-line edit that moves all of them together; there are no hardcoded `0xFF`
// masks and no bare `>> 8` shifts anywhere in the kernel or this module.
//
// It lives HERE, beside `NO_HANDLE`, because it defines what a handle word IS.
// The kernel's table and the wrappers' sentinel are both consequences of this
// encoding, and a consequence should not be where the rule is written.
//
// The budget being divided is 32 BITS on BOTH profiles — the portable width, so
// the wrap behaves identically on either machine and there is one story to test.
// A 64-bit word's top 32 bits stay zero, and `lookup_handle` enforces that
// rather than assuming it (see `handle_word_generation`).

/// The portable width a handle word is encoded in, on both profiles.
public static HANDLE_WORD_BITS: Int = 32

/// **THE ONE KNOB.** How many low bits of a handle word carry the table index;
/// the rest carry the generation. Default 8.
///
/// It is a SHIFT COUNT everywhere it is used, which is the one operand position
/// exempt from Saw's type-agreement rule, so it stays a plain `Int` and needs no
/// conversion at any use site.
public static HANDLE_INDEX_BITS: Int = 8

/// What is left of the portable budget for the generation counter.
public static HANDLE_GENERATION_BITS: Int = HANDLE_WORD_BITS - HANDLE_INDEX_BITS

/// The mask that takes the index field out of a handle word.
public static HANDLE_INDEX_MASK: UInt = (1 << HANDLE_INDEX_BITS) - 1

/// The generation counter's modulus — it WRAPS here, identically on both
/// profiles (D-1's "one story, one test").
///
/// It is also what makes the top-half-stays-zero rule ENFORCED rather than
/// assumed: a stored generation is always below this, and a word resolves by
/// comparing its whole generation FIELD against one, so a 64-bit word with
/// anything set above the portable budget has a generation no entry can equal
/// and names nothing. No separate range test is owed.
public static HANDLE_GENERATION_MODULUS: UInt32 = 1 << HANDLE_GENERATION_BITS

// THE KNOB'S OWN GUARD RAILS. Each field must exist, and the two must exactly
// divide the portable budget — a split that lost a bit would silently make some
// words unrepresentable, and one that overlapped would make a generation alias
// an index. The bound tying `MAX_HANDLES` to the index field is in
// `kcore.objects`, which is the module that knows both numbers.
static_assert(HANDLE_INDEX_BITS >= 1,
              "the index field must be at least one bit wide")
static_assert(HANDLE_GENERATION_BITS >= 1,
              "the generation field must be at least one bit wide")
static_assert(HANDLE_INDEX_BITS + HANDLE_GENERATION_BITS == HANDLE_WORD_BITS,
              "the index and generation fields must exactly divide the portable word")

/// The `entry` argument to `Process.ThreadCreate` that means "start the thread
/// at this process's own IMAGE ENTRY" — the entry the loader took out of the
/// image header.
///
/// It exists because of a LANGUAGE GAP, and the gap is worth stating where the
/// constant is: Saw cannot name the address of a function (design 172's reason
/// 3, DF-172a — `extern` declares functions, an extern function is not usable
/// as a value, and `@export` on a static emits a definition). So a process
/// written in Saw has no way to compute an entry address for a thread it wants
/// to create, and this is the one entry it can ask for by name. A process that
/// CAN produce an address — one with a C leaf, which is what the harness's
/// thread images use — passes it and the kernel checks it against the image's
/// granted region. See DF-178c.
public static ENTRY_IMAGE: UInt = 0

// =============================================================================
// Rights (spec §3)
// =============================================================================

// RIGHTS ARE SCOPED PER OBJECT KIND (ratified Aug 7). Each kind declares its
// own rights enum, so a bit means nothing until you know which kind's word it
// sits in — and dispatch establishes the kind before any right is checked (§3's
// order: table lookup -> object type -> op table -> rights check -> op). Two
// consequences worth stating plainly:
//
//   - Kind-specific bits OVERLAP freely across kinds. A rights word is only ever
//     interpreted against its handle's kind, so each kind owns 24 bits of its
//     own instead of all kinds sharing 32.
//   - The check is TYPED. `SystemObject.allows` takes a `SystemRight` and
//     nothing else, so testing a pipe right against a System handle is a
//     compile error rather than a bit comparison that quietly answers.
//
// THE UNIVERSAL LOW BYTE. Bits 0-7 are identical in EVERY kind's enum:
//
//   bit 0   Transfer   may be sent over a pipe — the movability capability
//   bit 1   Mint       may mint a SIBLING handle onto the same object
//   bits 2-7           RESERVED (candidates as they prove universal:
//                      wait/signal, introspect/info, revoke)
//
// **THE DOCTRINE, ruled Aug 29 (user), and it is what `Manage`'s removal made
// explicit: SPECIFIC RIGHTS FOR SPECIFIC OPERATIONS, PER OBJECT TYPE; UNIVERSAL
// BITS FOR UNIVERSAL OPS; THERE IS NO GENERIC AUTHORITY.** `Manage` held bit 1
// from M2 until this unit and meant "derive children" — a phrase general enough
// to be borrowed by four unrelated ops (`ProcessSelf`, `ThreadSelf`, `Give`, and
// a reading of sibling-derivation) and therefore specific enough to gate none of
// them. It is GONE, not renamed: every op it stood in front of now has a bit
// named after the op, and a launcher stripping one strips exactly one authority
// instead of an unpredictable bundle.
//
// **`Mint` TOOK ITS BIT** rather than sitting above it (vDSO discipline: numbers
// are not ABI, and a hole where a retired right used to be is a number nobody
// can read a reason for). It gates `MINT_OP`, the second universal op: a handle
// carrying it may produce a SIBLING onto the same object, in the caller's own
// table, with rights that are the source's INTERSECTED with a keep mask. That is
// a genuinely kind-independent authority — the op never learns the kind — which
// is what the universal byte is for, and it is the only bit besides `Transfer`
// that has earned a place in it.
//
// WHAT THE DOCTRINE DOES NOT MEAN: one bit per op NUMBER. `TimerRight.Arm` gates
// both `Arm` and `Disarm`, and `WaiterRight.Attach` gates both `Add` and
// `Remove`, because each pair is ONE capability — the power to decide when this
// timer fires, the power to decide what this Waiter watches — and splitting one
// would produce a right that withholds nothing (a holder who may arm can silence
// a timer by arming it a century out). The unit of a right is an AUTHORITY, and
// `Manage`'s failure was that "derive children" named no authority anybody could
// point at.
//
// A RIGHTS BIT IS SPELLED AS A SHIFT (ruled Aug 17, user; writable since
// DF-232c made a case value a const EXPRESSION). `case ClockGet = 1 << 10`, not
// `= 1024` — including the low bits, `Transfer = 1 << 0`, so the whole column
// reads as bit positions. The asserts below take the same spelling, which is
// what makes each one read as the rule it enforces: `>= (1 << 8)` IS
// "starts at bit 8".
//
// ONE THING ABOUT THE ASSERT SPELLING, and it is easy to get wrong: the
// PARENTHESES ARE LOAD-BEARING — Saw's bitwise tier binds BELOW comparison, so
// a bare `x >= 1 << 8` is `(x >= 1) << 8`. The `u32` suffix these asserts used
// to carry is GONE (DF-243a, fixed Aug 22): a const expression adopts the other
// operand's width in a mixed binop exactly as a bare literal does, so one bit is
// spelled one way here — as a shift, in the case value and in the assert that
// checks it. The numbers never moved.
//
// Six bits are reserved rather than assigned because more shared operations are
// coming and a universal right added later must not have to move a kind's bits
// to make room. Generic kernel paths — pipe handle-transfer is the first —
// check universal bits without knowing the kind, which is only sound while the
// table holds, so the `static_assert`s below hold it: they are what stops a kind
// enum from drifting, and what catches a kind-specific right placed below bit 8.

/// A right on the System object.
///
/// The low byte is the universal table above; System's own rights start at bit
/// 8. `Debug` gates the console, `Shutdown` gates stopping the machine — a
/// process holding the handle without them can name the object and do nothing
/// with it, which is the point of checking rights per-OP rather than per-object.
///
/// **`ProcessSelf` IS NEW IN M3 UNIT 3, and it exists because `Manage` does
/// not** (the Aug-29 doctrine: specific rights for specific operations). Asking
/// the System object which Process you are was gated by `Manage` from M2, on a
/// reading of "derive children" that died when universal `Mint` took over
/// sibling-derivation and left the word meaning nothing in particular. It is a
/// specific authority with its own substitution seam, exactly as `ClockGet` is:
/// a launcher that strips this bit hands a child a System handle through which
/// it can print and tell the time and never learn its own identity — and a
/// launcher that later wants to SUPPLY that identity some other way (a virtual
/// process object over IPC, the same move `ClockGet` describes for a virtual
/// clock) has a bit to strip rather than an op to intercept.
/// `ClockGet` gates the one op that hands out a time source, and it is a right
/// rather than an ambient facility for a reason worth stating where the bit is
/// (design 232 unit 1; the M3 time notes): TIME IS A GRANTED CAPABILITY. A
/// launcher that strips this bit from a child's System handle has a process
/// that cannot read the machine's clock at all — which is what makes handing it
/// a VIRTUAL clock over IPC later a substitution rather than a rewrite. It is
/// deliberately NOT in the stripped-by-construction set the M3 device rulings
/// describe (`Shutdown` and the bind/alloc ops): an ordinary process needs to
/// tell the time, and a clock grants authority over nothing.
///
/// `ProcessCreate` is the newest bit, and WHY IT IS HERE rather than on Process
/// is the Aug-29 user ruling recorded as sawos design 2's RIDER: process-scoped
/// objects are minted by your Process; PROCESSES ARE MINTED BY THE SYSTEM. The
/// process table is a machine-global resource bounded by machine facts — the
/// slab today, the protection-domain reloads, ASIDs when Profile B grows real
/// translation — so its refusals and its limits are SYSTEM answers, while
/// threads, events, waiters and timers stay a process's own. `ProcessSelf` is
/// the precedent that was already sitting here: Process handles have always
/// come from System, and now they all do. A launcher that is handed a System
/// handle stripped of this bit cannot spawn — which is the attenuation the old
/// placement only appeared to offer, since with a root/launcher topology a
/// creation right on the CALLER's own Process handle gates nothing real.
public enum SystemRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Debug = 1 << 8,
    case Shutdown = 1 << 9,
    case ClockGet = 1 << 10,
    case ProcessCreate = 1 << 11,
    case ProcessSelf = 1 << 12,
}

// The universal low byte, pinned. Every kind's enum repeats these two asserts
// verbatim; that repetition IS the check, since it is what makes a drifted bit
// fail to build rather than fail in a pipe transfer years later.
static_assert((SystemRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((SystemRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")

// Kind-specific rights start at bit 8, leaving bits 2-7 free for universal
// rights not yet assigned. One assert per kind-specific case.
static_assert((SystemRight.Debug as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Debug must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((SystemRight.Shutdown as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Shutdown must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((SystemRight.ClockGet as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right ClockGet must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((SystemRight.ProcessCreate as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right ProcessCreate must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((SystemRight.ProcessSelf as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right ProcessSelf must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Process object (§2 table row; design 178 D4).
///
/// `ThreadSelf` gates one thing: asking the process for the CALLING THREAD's
/// handle. It was `Manage`'s from M2 until the Aug-29 doctrine retired that word
/// — specific rights for specific operations — and it is now named for its op
/// like every other bit here. What it buys a launcher is what `ProcessSelf` buys
/// one object up: a child that may not learn which thread it is, withheld by a
/// bit rather than by a second mechanism.
///
/// `Kill` is deliberately ABSENT. Spec §8 ratifies it as the capability-gated
/// escape hatch userspace supervision needs against a wedged process — ANOTHER
/// process. With one process in the system it is `Exit` under a second name,
/// and an op whose only reachable use is degenerate is an op nothing tests. It
/// lands with M3's multi-process loading, beside `process_create`.
///
/// THE TWO M3-UNIT-2 BITS, and each one gates a DIFFERENT ROLE the Process
/// object now plays (sawos design 2 D-4, as amended by its RIDER):
///
///   - `Start` is a RECEIVER authority on the CHILD's handle — the second
///     phase of the two-phase lifecycle, and the reason it is its own bit is
///     that a supervisor may legitimately hold a child's handle to WATCH it
///     (`Wait`) without being allowed to run it.
///   - `BootHandles` gates the boot-set iterator. Root holds it because root is
///     the process the kernel minted the boot set FOR; what a CHILD gets is
///     unit 3's question, and a bit withheld here is the whole of the answer.
///
/// THERE WAS A THIRD, AND IT MOVED. `ProcessCreate` sat here as a factory
/// authority on the CALLER's own Process handle until the Aug-29 user ruling
/// (sawos design 2's RIDER) put it on System: process-scoped objects are minted
/// by your Process; PROCESSES ARE MINTED BY THE SYSTEM. It is now
/// `SystemRight.ProcessCreate`, and `Start`/`BootHandles` renumbered DOWN into
/// the hole it left — legal because `kernel/sysapi` is vDSO discipline and
/// these numbers are not ABI: userspace names the case, the module supplies the
/// value, and the two are compiled together in one image.
///
/// `EventCreate` and `WaiterCreate` are M2 unit 3's, and where they sit ANSWERS
/// an open pin: spec §12 leaves the creation-authority model for plain objects
/// (Pipe/Event/Waiter/Timer) open between quota-gated free creation and a
/// factory capability. This is the factory capability, and it is one a process
/// already holds — the Process object is what an Event or a Waiter is CHARGED
/// to (its slab slot, its handle-table entry, its teardown), so making the
/// authority to create one flow through the Process handle is §3's derivation
/// rule rather than a new mechanism beside it. A quota is additive on top: it
/// would be a field on the process slot, checked where `NoResource` is returned
/// today. Nothing here forecloses it.
///
/// ONE BIT PER CREATION OP rather than a single `Create`, matching
/// `ThreadCreate`. The bits are what attenuation strips, so an object kind a
/// process may not make is a bit it was not given — collapsing them would make
/// "may create Events" and "may create Waiters" the same capability, which they
/// are not. Twenty-four bits are available and eight are used.
///
/// `InterruptBind` is the one whose absence MATTERS today, and it is worth
/// saying why the same shape carries a much stronger authority: an Interrupt
/// BINDS AN IRQ LINE, so the bit is what separates a driver process from an
/// ordinary one. In M2 root holds it because root is the only process there is;
/// M3's launcher strips it from everything it spawns except the drivers, which
/// is §3's monotonic attenuation doing exactly what it is for.
/// **`Map` IS THE UNIT-4 BIT, AND IT IS AN AUTHORITY OVER *THIS* PROCESS**
/// (sawos design 6 D-3). `Memory.Map` / `IoMemory.Map` name a target Process as
/// their argument, and installing a protection row into somebody's domain is
/// not something possession of a region should imply — the doctrine, again: no
/// possession-implies-authority. So a map spends TWO rights on two different
/// objects, `MemoryRight.Map` on the region and this bit on the process being
/// mapped into, and a launcher that hands a child a region without this bit on
/// the child's handle has handed over bytes it cannot install anywhere.
///
/// It sits beside `Give` for the same reason `Give` sits beside `Start`: they
/// are separable authorities over one child. A builder that may furnish a
/// child's boot set need not be allowed to edit its address space, and a
/// supervisor that may run and watch need not be allowed either.
///
/// **`Give` IS ITS OWN BIT** (M3 unit 3, as amended by the Aug-29 doctrine). An
/// earlier cut of this unit gated the courier op on `Manage`, arguing that
/// furnishing a process you created is management OF it; the doctrine refused
/// the move on its own terms — "management" is exactly the generic word that
/// gates nothing — and the authority has a perfectly good name of its own: MAY
/// PLACE HANDLES INTO THIS CHILD'S BOOT SET. It sits beside `Start` rather than
/// inside it because the two are genuinely separable: a supervisor may hold a
/// child's handle to run and watch it without being allowed to furnish it, and a
/// builder may furnish without being allowed to run.
///
/// **`PipeCreate` IS M4 UNIT 1's BIT, AND WHY IT IS *HERE* IS A RULING** (sawos
/// design 10 ruling 3, built by design 13). The factory for a connection sits on
/// the PROCESS object, like `EventCreate` and `WaiterCreate` and unlike
/// `ProcessCreate` — pipes are IPC and carry nothing system-shaped, so the
/// System lean was REJECTED. What that buys is the thing a machine-global
/// factory could not offer without a mask: the authority is PER-PROCESS
/// ATTENUABLE. A launcher that hands a child a Process handle minted through a
/// keep mask without this bit has a child that can hold and use connections
/// somebody else made and can never make one of its own.
///
/// It joins the ONE default set below, on the same terms `EventCreate` did: the
/// Process object is what a connection is CHARGED to (its slab slot, its two
/// handle entries, its quota row), so the authority to make one flows through
/// the handle that is charged — §3's derivation rule rather than a new mechanism
/// beside it.
///
/// **`Stats` IS DESIGN 16's BIT, AND IT IS AN AUTHORITY TO OBSERVE** (sawos
/// design 16, user-ruled Sep 1). It gates `ProcessOp.Stats` — a per-kind right
/// named for its op, the Aug-29 doctrine — and it is worth saying what makes
/// reading three counters worth a bit of its own rather than riding `Wait`, the
/// other observation bit. `Wait` answers §8's status word, which is a fact about
/// whether the process is ALIVE; this answers how many traps it has taken, which
/// is a fact about what it has been DOING. A supervisor that may learn a child
/// died need not be able to watch it work, and the separation costs one bit.
///
/// It joins the ONE default set below on the terms every other kind-specific bit
/// joined it on: self-inspection has to compose for free (`ProcessSelf` ->
/// `proc.stats()`, which is the whole point of the op), and attenuating it away
/// from a child is a launcher's keep mask rather than a second default list.
public enum ProcessRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case ThreadCreate = 1 << 8,
    case Exit = 1 << 9,
    case Wait = 1 << 10,
    case EventCreate = 1 << 11,
    case WaiterCreate = 1 << 12,
    case InterruptBind = 1 << 13,
    case Start = 1 << 14,
    case BootHandles = 1 << 15,
    case ThreadSelf = 1 << 16,
    case Give = 1 << 17,
    case Map = 1 << 18,
    case PipeCreate = 1 << 19,
    case Stats = 1 << 20,
}

static_assert((ProcessRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ProcessRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ProcessRight.ThreadCreate as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right ThreadCreate must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.Exit as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Exit must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.EventCreate as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right EventCreate must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.WaiterCreate as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right WaiterCreate must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.InterruptBind as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right InterruptBind must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.Start as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Start must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.BootHandles as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right BootHandles must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.ThreadSelf as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right ThreadSelf must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.Give as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Give must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.Map as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Map must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.PipeCreate as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right PipeCreate must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.Stats as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Stats must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Thread object (§2 table row; design 178 D4).
///
/// The split is between what an OWNER of a thread may do to it — `Start` it,
/// `Join` it — and what a thread does to ITSELF, which is `Control`: exit, and
/// yield the processor. Both of the latter check that the caller IS the named
/// thread, so `Control` on someone else's handle still cannot drive it; the
/// right is what makes the check worth running rather than the check itself.
public enum ThreadRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Start = 1 << 8,
    case Join = 1 << 9,
    case Control = 1 << 10,
}

static_assert((ThreadRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ThreadRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ThreadRight.Start as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Start must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ThreadRight.Join as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Join must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ThreadRight.Control as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Control must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on an Event object (§2.4; design 178 D5).
///
/// THE THREE ARE THE THREE ENDS OF A NOTIFICATION and they attenuate apart on
/// purpose. `Signal` is the producer's — it is the whole of what a driver or a
/// peer needs, and a handle carrying only this bit can raise the event and can
/// never learn anything from it. `Receive` is the consumer's: it drains the
/// word and resets it. `Wait` is the right to have READINESS OBSERVED — what
/// attaching to a Waiter spends — and it is separate from `Receive` because a
/// dispatcher that parks on a set of events need not be the code that reads any
/// of them.
///
/// `Wait` IS A CONSUMING RIGHT TOO, since the Aug-17 ruling: a wait's record
/// carries the accumulated word and the delivery clears it, so a handle holding
/// only this bit takes values as well as observes them. That was always half
/// true — the record has carried the word since M2 — and what changed is that it
/// now empties the object as well. The two bits therefore attenuate by DOOR
/// (park or poll) rather than by whether the holder learns anything, and a
/// launcher that wants a pure readiness observer has no bit for it. Nothing in
/// SOS needs one yet: with no handle transfer before M4, every right on an Event
/// is held by the process that made it.
///
/// `Mint` is granted at creation on the uniform lean the default-sets section
/// records: a second NAME for an Event costs nothing and can itself be
/// attenuated, where withholding it would only mean the object cannot be handed
/// on in narrowed form. (`Manage` used to be withheld here on the argument that
/// an Event derives nothing; the Aug-29 doctrine retired the bit entirely.)
public enum EventRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Signal = 1 << 8,
    case Receive = 1 << 9,
    case Wait = 1 << 10,
}

static_assert((EventRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((EventRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((EventRight.Signal as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Signal must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((EventRight.Receive as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Receive must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((EventRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Waiter object (§2.2; design 178 D5).
///
/// `Attach` gates BOTH `Add` and `Remove`, which is one bit for what reads like
/// two operations — deliberately, because they are one capability: the power to
/// decide what a Waiter watches. A holder who may add may already replace the
/// whole attachment set by adding and never removing, so splitting the pair
/// would produce a right that withholds nothing.
///
/// `Wait` is the right to PARK on it, and it is separate because the two roles
/// genuinely divide: §2.2 ratifies many threads waiting on one Waiter, so a
/// pool of workers holds `Wait` while whoever builds the attachment set holds
/// `Attach`.
public enum WaiterRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Attach = 1 << 8,
    case Wait = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((WaiterRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((WaiterRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((WaiterRight.Attach as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Attach must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((WaiterRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on an Interrupt object (§2 table; spec §9; design 178 D6).
///
/// TWO RIGHTS, AND THEY ARE THE TWO HALVES OF spec §9's CYCLE. `Wait` is the
/// right to have the line's assertion OBSERVED — what attaching to a Waiter
/// spends, the same meaning `EventRight.Wait` has — and `Ack` is the right to
/// END a fire, which is what unmasks the line and lets the device interrupt
/// again. They attenuate apart for a real shape: a supervisor that watches a
/// driver's lines holds `Wait` alone and can never rearm one, which is exactly
/// the authority a monitor should have.
///
/// There is deliberately no `Mask` right and no mask op. Masking is the
/// KERNEL's half of the cycle (§9's mask-on-fire), not something a driver
/// asks for — a driver that could mask a line at will could also leave it
/// masked, which is a denial of the device with no diagnostic. What a driver
/// controls is its own device's interrupt-enable register, through the window
/// it was granted, which is where that decision belongs.
///
/// `Mint` is granted at creation on the uniform lean the default-sets section
/// records. (`Manage` used to be withheld here, on the argument that an
/// Interrupt derives nothing; the bit no longer exists.)
public enum InterruptRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Wait = 1 << 8,
    case Ack = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((InterruptRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((InterruptRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((InterruptRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((InterruptRight.Ack as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Ack must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Clock object (§2 table; design 232 unit 1).
///
/// THE TWO SPLIT READING TIME FROM SCHEDULING IT, and they attenuate apart for
/// a shape that already exists: a process that only ever stamps a log line
/// holds `Read` alone and can never consume a Timer slot, while a dispatcher
/// that arms timeouts holds both. Neither is the other's subset in practice —
/// `TimerCreate` without `Read` is a process that sleeps by the clock without
/// being able to name what time it is, which is exactly what a pure event loop
/// does.
///
/// `Mint` is granted at creation on the uniform lean the default-sets section
/// records. (`Manage` used to be withheld here, on the argument that a Clock's
/// only derivation IS a Timer and that already has its own bit — which is the
/// Aug-29 doctrine arriving early, about the very bit it retired.)
public enum ClockRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Read = 1 << 8,
    case TimerCreate = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((ClockRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ClockRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ClockRight.Read as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Read must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ClockRight.TimerCreate as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right TimerCreate must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Timer object (§2 table; design 232 unit 1).
///
/// `Arm` GATES BOTH `Arm` AND `Disarm`, which is one bit for what reads like two
/// operations — the same call `WaiterRight.Attach` makes, and for the same
/// reason: they are one capability, the power to decide when this timer fires.
/// A holder who may arm can already silence a timer by arming it a century out,
/// so splitting the pair would produce a right that withholds nothing.
///
/// `Wait` is the right to have readiness OBSERVED — what attaching to a Waiter
/// spends, the same meaning it has on an Event and an Interrupt — and it is
/// separate because the roles genuinely divide: a supervisor parked on a set of
/// timeouts holds `Wait` alone and can never reschedule one.
///
/// `Mint` is granted at creation on the uniform lean the default-sets section
/// records. (`Manage` used to be withheld here; the bit no longer exists.)
public enum TimerRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Arm = 1 << 8,
    case Wait = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((TimerRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((TimerRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((TimerRight.Arm as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Arm must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((TimerRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Memory object (spec §2.5; sawos design 2 D-2, grown by design
/// 6 D-2/D-3).
///
/// `Transfer` is unit 3's `give` — a region is exactly the kind of thing a
/// launcher hands to a child — and `Mint` is unit 3's attenuated sibling. Both
/// were minted at boot before anything read either, because attenuation is
/// monotonic: a bit not minted at boot could never appear later.
///
/// **THE TWO UNIT-4 BITS ARE NAMED FOR THEIR OPS**, per the Aug-29 doctrine —
/// which is exactly what unit 2's docstring promised would happen here instead
/// of a generic `Manage` (the bit that used to sit at 1 << 1 for that borrowed
/// purpose, and is gone):
///
///   - `Split` is the authority to DERIVE — one cut from the front, the parent
///     becoming the remainder. A pool root a launcher hands out with this bit
///     withheld is a fixed range and nothing else, which is the honest way to
///     say "here is your RAM, you may not subdivide it for anyone".
///   - `Map` is the authority to INSTALL this region in some domain. It is
///     half of what a map spends: `ProcessRight.Map` on the target process is
///     the other half, because possession of bytes must not imply authority
///     over an address space.
///
/// There is STILL deliberately NO `Read`/`Write` pair, and unit 4 is what makes
/// the reason visible rather than merely stated: access is a property of the
/// MAPPING (`map(access:)`), which is precisely what lets one region be mapped
/// read-only in one place and read-write in another. Rights on the region could
/// not express that without becoming per-mapping, at which point they would be
/// the mapping's.
///
/// **AND `MapExecute` IS THE ONE EXCEPTION TO THAT PARAGRAPH, RULED AUG 30**
/// (sawos design 9 D-1). X stays a property of the MAPPING — the sentence above
/// is untouched, and a region carries no R/W/X triple — but WHICH access bits a
/// handle may REQUEST is now itself an authority. `MemoryOp.Map` refuses
/// `MapAccess.Execute` unless the handle carries this bit, which is the same
/// refusal every unspent right answers.
///
/// **IT IS CAPABILITY FLOW, NOT IDENTITY.** "Only root maps executable" holds
/// because root never GRANTS the bit — it mints an attenuated sibling and gives
/// that — and not because the kernel asks who is calling. A launcher that hands
/// a driver child its RAM through a mint whose keep mask omits `MapExecute` has
/// handed over memory that child can read and write and can never execute from,
/// stated once at the mint rather than checked at every map.
///
/// **WHY A FOURTH BIT RATHER THAN A W^X RULE ALONE**: the two answer different
/// questions. W^X (below, in `MapAccess`) says no single ROW may be both
/// writable and executable, which every process may still satisfy by
/// double-mapping. This bit says whether a process may produce an executable row
/// AT ALL. A system that only had W^X would let any holder of a region build
/// itself a JIT; a system that only had this bit would let a privileged holder
/// build a W|X row. Both, and each is one comparison.
///
/// **STATED HONESTLY: THIS IS PER-HANDLE, NOT PER-REGION.** A second handle onto
/// the same bytes that still carries the bit can map them executable — the
/// aliasing stance design 6 took (no aliasing bookkeeping on a machine that does
/// not translate) is unchanged, and a region-level immutable flag was REJECTED
/// as a second mechanism doing overlapping work.
public enum MemoryRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Split = 1 << 8,
    case Map = 1 << 9,
    case MapExecute = 1 << 10,
}

static_assert((MemoryRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((MemoryRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((MemoryRight.Split as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Split must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((MemoryRight.Map as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Map must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((MemoryRight.MapExecute as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right MapExecute must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on an IoMemory object (spec §2.5; sawos design 6 D-1/D-2).
///
/// **THE SAME TWO SHAPES AS `MemoryRight`, WITH THE DERIVATION VERB CHANGED**,
/// and the change of verb is the whole difference between the kinds. `Carve`
/// takes a sub-window and LEAVES THE PARENT ALONE — §2.5's "a fixed region may
/// be handed out many times" — where `Split` consumes what it hands out. There
/// is no bit that means both, because a holder of one authority is not a holder
/// of the other: a driver handed a carve right over a peripheral's window can
/// produce views for its own sub-devices without ever being able to take a
/// range away from anyone.
///
/// `Map` means what it means on a Memory and spends the same second right
/// (`ProcessRight.Map`) on the target. What differs is not the authority but the
/// ROW it installs: an IoMemory can only ever produce a device-attribute row,
/// which is the type-level guarantee `ObjType.IoMemory` exists for.
///
/// `Transfer` is here because handing a driver its window is the whole point of
/// unit 6's flow, and `Mint` on the uniform lean the default-sets section
/// records. **UNIT 6 SPENT IT** (sawos design 9 D-2): `Process.give(iomemory:)`
/// moves the console's register page into a driver child's table, and the child
/// maps it into itself. There is deliberately no `MapExecute` bit beside `Map`
/// here — `IoMemoryOp.Map` takes no access argument at all, so the authority
/// design 9 added to `MemoryRight` has nothing to gate on this kind.
public enum IoMemoryRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Carve = 1 << 8,
    case Map = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((IoMemoryRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((IoMemoryRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((IoMemoryRight.Carve as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Carve must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((IoMemoryRight.Map as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Map must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Mapping object (spec §2.5; sawos design 6 D-1/D-3).
///
/// **ONE KIND-SPECIFIC BIT, BECAUSE THE OBJECT HAS ONE OP.** `Unmap` is the
/// authority to take the row out, and §2.5's "only the Mapping handle can
/// unmap" is what makes holding one meaningful at all.
///
/// **`Transfer` IS DECLARED AND NEVER MINTED**, which is the one thing worth
/// reading here. The bit exists because the universal low byte is pinned in
/// every kind's enum by the asserts below — that repetition IS the check — but
/// `mapping_rights()` does not grant it, so a Mapping cannot be given and
/// `BootHandleKind` has no case for one. The reason is the object's own
/// meaning: a Mapping names a row in a SPECIFIC process's domain, so handing
/// one to a third party would be handing over the power to edit an address
/// space that party was never given authority over. Revocation across a
/// process boundary is a supervision story, and it wants a ruling rather than a
/// bit granted by default.
public enum MappingRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Unmap = 1 << 8,
}

static_assert((MappingRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((MappingRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((MappingRight.Unmap as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Unmap must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a PipeInlet — the CLIENT end of a connection (spec §2.1; sawos
/// design 13, on design 10 ruling 2).
///
/// **ONE KIND-SPECIFIC BIT IN UNIT 1, AND IT IS NAMED FOR ITS OP** (the Aug-29
/// doctrine): `Post` gates the one submission op the kernel's client side has.
/// Ruling 4 is why there is exactly one — `post` IS the primitive, and blocking
/// send, timeout and TELL are all LIBRARY compositions over it, so there is no
/// second door for a bit to gate.
///
/// **THE `Wait` BIT ARRIVED WITH WAITABILITY** (M4 unit 3, sawos design 15). Its
/// absence through units 1 and 2 was a scope line rather than a decision —
/// attaching an endpoint to a Waiter spends a right, and which right that is
/// could not be answered before there was a readiness to observe — and
/// `waitable_slot`'s `NotWaitable` arms for both kinds are gone.
///
/// `Transfer` is minted because handing a child one end of a connection is the
/// whole point of the pair (design 13's `pipe-child` proof), and `Mint` on the
/// uniform lean the default-sets section records — a second NAME for an endpoint
/// is a second REFERENCE on that endpoint's column, which is what makes "any
/// handle to an end holds that end open" (D-1's v1 rule, the Unix dup
/// precedent) true by construction rather than by a check.
public enum PipeInletRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Post = 1 << 8,
    /// **THE SECOND KIND-SPECIFIC BIT, AND M4 UNIT 3 IS WHERE IT ARRIVED**
    /// (sawos design 15). It gates attaching this kind to a Waiter, on the
    /// Timer precedent — the OUTLET's readable level and this end's own room-to-post level are two different waitables; this bit is the one that gates attaching THIS end (design 10 ruling 8). Its absence through unit 2 was a scope line and
    /// this is that line being crossed, not a widening of anything: the bit is
    /// in the default set for the reason every kind-specific bit is, which is
    /// that attenuation is monotonic and a bit not minted at creation could
    /// never appear later.
    case Wait = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((PipeInletRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((PipeInletRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((PipeInletRight.Post as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Post must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((PipeInletRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a PipeOutlet — the SERVER end of a connection (spec §2.1; sawos
/// design 13).
///
/// The mirror of `PipeInletRight` with the verb changed, and the change of verb
/// is the whole difference between the ends: `Take` gates the one op that reads
/// a staged message out. Everything `PipeInletRight` says about the missing
/// `Wait` bit, about `Transfer` and about `Mint` holds here word for word.
///
/// **THE BITS OVERLAP `PipeInletRight`'s AND THAT IS SOUND** — a rights word is
/// only ever interpreted against its handle's KIND, which §3's order establishes
/// first, so `Post` and `Take` sharing bit 8 is the per-kind scoping working
/// rather than a collision. Testing an inlet's right against an outlet is a
/// COMPILE error, not a bit comparison that quietly answers.
public enum PipeOutletRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Take = 1 << 8,
    /// **THE SECOND KIND-SPECIFIC BIT, AND M4 UNIT 3 IS WHERE IT ARRIVED**
    /// (sawos design 15). It gates attaching this kind to a Waiter, on the
    /// Timer precedent — attaching the SERVER end to a Waiter is what a multiplexed server does instead of polling `Take` (spec §2.2's waitable list). Its absence through unit 2 was a scope line and
    /// this is that line being crossed, not a widening of anything: the bit is
    /// in the default set for the reason every kind-specific bit is, which is
    /// that attenuation is monotonic and a bit not minted at creation could
    /// never appear later.
    case Wait = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((PipeOutletRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((PipeOutletRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((PipeOutletRight.Take as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Take must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((PipeOutletRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a PipeReply — the CLIENT's claim on one reply (spec §2.1; sawos
/// design 14).
///
/// **ONE KIND-SPECIFIC BIT, NAMED FOR ITS OP** (the Aug-29 doctrine): `Resolve`
/// gates the one op a claim carries. **THE `Wait` BIT — the reply-ready level
/// §2.2 owed — ARRIVED WITH WAITABILITY** (M4 unit 3, sawos design 15), and it
/// is the sharpest of the four: a claim's delivery carries the reply's own
/// BYTES, so attaching one replaces resolving it.
///
/// `Transfer` is minted because a claim is a capability like any other and §2.1
/// ratifies both one-shots as transferable. `Mint` is minted on the uniform lean
/// the endpoint pair follows, and it composes with single-use rather than
/// fighting it: a sibling is a second NAME on the reply column, the op consumes
/// an ENTRY, and the EXCHANGE's own state is what allows exactly one successful
/// resolve. A sibling that resolves second meets `PeerClosed`, not a second
/// reply.
public enum PipeReplyRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Resolve = 1 << 8,
    /// **THE SECOND KIND-SPECIFIC BIT, AND M4 UNIT 3 IS WHERE IT ARRIVED**
    /// (sawos design 15). It gates attaching this kind to a Waiter, on the
    /// Timer precedent — attaching a CLAIM is how a client waits for one answer among many — and since ruling 11(a) the delivery carries the answer itself, so the attach can replace the resolve entirely. Its absence through unit 2 was a scope line and
    /// this is that line being crossed, not a widening of anything: the bit is
    /// in the default set for the reason every kind-specific bit is, which is
    /// that attenuation is monotonic and a bit not minted at creation could
    /// never appear later.
    case Wait = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((PipeReplyRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((PipeReplyRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((PipeReplyRight.Resolve as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Resolve must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((PipeReplyRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a PipeRequest — the SERVER's obligation to send one reply (spec
/// §2.1; sawos design 14).
///
/// The mirror of `PipeReplyRight` with the verb changed, and the change of verb
/// is the whole difference between the two halves of a pair: a claim resolves,
/// an obligation replies. Everything `PipeReplyRight` says about the `Wait` bit
/// (here it gates the request-abandoned level — §2.1's ratified early notice),
/// about `Transfer` — here it is §2.1's DELEGATION PRIMITIVE rather than a
/// convenience — and about `Mint` holds word for word.
///
/// The bits overlap `PipeReplyRight`'s and that is sound for the reason the
/// endpoint pair's do: a rights word is only ever read against its handle's
/// KIND, which §3's order establishes first.
public enum PipeRequestRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Reply = 1 << 8,
    /// **THE SECOND KIND-SPECIFIC BIT, AND M4 UNIT 3 IS WHERE IT ARRIVED**
    /// (sawos design 15). It gates attaching this kind to a Waiter, on the
    /// Timer precedent — attaching an OBLIGATION is the server's cancellation watcher: §2.1's ratified "the `PipeRequestHandle` is WAITABLE for abandoned". Its absence through unit 2 was a scope line and
    /// this is that line being crossed, not a widening of anything: the bit is
    /// in the default set for the reason every kind-specific bit is, which is
    /// that attenuation is monotonic and a bit not minted at creation could
    /// never appear later.
    case Wait = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((PipeRequestRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((PipeRequestRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((PipeRequestRight.Reply as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Reply must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((PipeRequestRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")

/// Whether a rights word carries the UNIVERSAL `Transfer` bit (§3's low byte).
///
/// **IT TAKES A PLAIN MASK RATHER THAN A TYPED RIGHT, and that is the point**
/// (sawos design 4 D-1). `ProcessOp.Give` checks this bit on the handle BEING
/// GIVEN, which may be any kind at all — so the check happens at the one
/// altitude that has the entry and has not branched on its `obj_type`, exactly
/// as `RELEASE_OP` does. A typed `allows` cannot be written there, and writing
/// nine of them would be nine spellings of one universal bit.
///
/// `SystemRight.Transfer` is the spelling and ANY kind's would do: the
/// `static_assert`s above pin all ten enums' `Transfer` to bit 0, and that
/// repetition is what makes this function's one reading safe. This is the first
/// consumer of the universal low byte the §3 table has ever had — the bit was
/// declared in M2 and read by nothing until give.
public func rights_allow_transfer(rights: UInt32) -> Bool {
    (rights & (SystemRight.Transfer as UInt32)) != 0
}

/// Whether a rights word carries the UNIVERSAL `Mint` bit (§3's low byte, bit 2).
///
/// The same shape and the same reasoning as `rights_allow_transfer` above:
/// `MINT_OP` is intercepted BEFORE the kind match, so the check has the entry and
/// not the kind and a typed `allows` cannot be written there. `SystemRight.Mint`
/// is the spelling and any kind's would do — the `static_assert`s pin every kind to
/// bit 2.
public func rights_allow_mint(rights: UInt32) -> Bool {
    (rights & (SystemRight.Mint as UInt32)) != 0
}

// =============================================================================
// WHAT EVERY DEFAULT SET MINTS (spec §3)
// =============================================================================
//
// **`Mint` IS IN EVERY ONE OF THEM, UNIFORMLY** (the Aug-29 user rulings on
// design 4, ruling 3 — recorded as the LEAD's lean, which the user may veto at
// integration). The argument for uniformity is that the alternative is a
// per-kind table nobody can predict from the outside: a handle either can name
// its object twice or cannot, and making that vary by kind would mean a launcher
// has to know which kinds are mintable before it can write a policy.
//
// **WHAT IT COSTS IS NOTHING, AND THAT IS THE POINT.** A mint AMPLIFIES NOTHING
// (§3): the rights it produces are the source's INTERSECTED with a keep mask, so
// the sibling is always a subset, and the authority to mint is itself a bit a
// mask can omit — a launcher hands out a handle whose mask left `Mint` out and
// the proliferation stops there. A default that withheld `Mint` would only mean
// "this kind cannot be attenuated", which is a strictly worse system: the
// alternative to minting an attenuated sibling is handing over the full-rights
// original.
//
// Whether `Mint` should be withheld anywhere is therefore a per-kind ruling that
// nothing has needed yet, exactly as `Transfer`'s placement is.

/// What the kernel mints for root's System handle at boot (§12).
///
/// A function rather than a `static`, and THE ORIGINAL REASON HAS EXPIRED: a
/// static initializer folds const expressions since design 186 (an OR of
/// raw-backed enum cases among them, DF-240a), so this body would be a legal
/// static initializer today. The function is kept as it is anyway — converting
/// it is a real change nobody has ruled on, and the spelling was never the
/// point. What is: `768` with a comment saying which bits it is made of is
/// exactly the magic number this file is getting rid of.
///
/// Root gets both System operations plus the three DERIVATION bits its boot set
/// is a SEED of rather than the whole of: `ProcessSelf` (ask the System object
/// which Process you are), `ClockGet` (ask for a time source) and `Mint`. Every
/// later object arrives through a handle already held (§3's derivation rule)
/// rather than through a register the kernel had to widen.
///
/// **`ProcessSelf` REPLACED `Manage` HERE IN M3 UNIT 3** (the Aug-29 doctrine:
/// specific rights for specific operations). The bit is the same authority under
/// a name that says which op it gates; nothing root can do changed.
///
/// **`Transfer` IS HERE SINCE M3 UNIT 3, AND THE M2 REASON FOR ITS ABSENCE IS
/// SUPERSEDED** (user ruling, Aug 29). M2 withheld it saying "there is nobody to
/// transfer a handle to in a one-process world"; unit 2 ENDED that world and
/// this unit acts on it. What the bit buys is the launch flow's last missing
/// piece: root MINTS a masked sibling of its System handle — `Debug |
/// ProcessSelf | ClockGet | Transfer`, with `Shutdown`, `ProcessCreate` and
/// `Mint` absent BY SILENCE from the keep mask — and GIVES that to a child. So a
/// child bootstraps EXACTLY AS ROOT DOES: a System handle in its first argument
/// register, its own Process handle derived from it, and its boot set drained
/// from there (§12's symmetry). It can print without owning a device, tell the
/// time, and neither stop the machine nor spawn.
///
/// Note which handle travels: never THIS one. Root gives a SIBLING a keep mask
/// has already narrowed, and what a child receives cannot be re-widened, since a
/// mint's result is a subset of its source.
///
/// **THE MASK MUST KEEP `Transfer`, AND THAT IS A RECORDED FINDING** rather than
/// a choice: `give` checks the universal `Transfer` bit on the handle BEING
/// GIVEN, so a launcher cannot narrow BELOW the ticket that lets the narrowed
/// thing arrive. A child therefore holds `Transfer` on its System handle and
/// could pass it on — inert today, since it has no `ProcessCreate` to make a
/// receiver with and no pipe to send over until M4. Closing it wants a ruling (a
/// give that CONSUMES the bit, or a strip-on-arrival rule), and either would
/// amend design 4 D-1's rights-VERBATIM sentence, so neither is taken here.
///
/// `ProcessCreate` joins in M3 unit 2, by design 2's RIDER (Aug 29): processes
/// are minted by the SYSTEM, so root's authority to launch is a bit on the one
/// handle the entry register carries. A launcher handed a System handle without
/// it cannot spawn — which, with a root/launcher topology, is the only place
/// that refusal could have been made to mean anything.
///
/// Attenuation is monotonic, so a right withheld here can never appear later —
/// which is the right default for a set the boot handle hands out.
public func root_system_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((SystemRight.Debug as UInt32) | (SystemRight.Shutdown as UInt32)
     | (SystemRight.ProcessSelf as UInt32) | (SystemRight.ClockGet as UInt32)
     | (SystemRight.ProcessCreate as UInt32) | (SystemRight.Mint as UInt32)
     | (SystemRight.Transfer as UInt32))
}

/// **THE Process DEFAULT SET — ONE SET, THREE MINTERS** (§12; design 178 D4;
/// design 2 D-4; re-ruled by design 4 D-3 and collapsed by the Aug-29 doctrine).
///
/// The three places a Process handle is minted — root's at boot, the one
/// `SystemOp.ProcessSelf` hands a process for itself, and the one
/// `SystemOp.ProcessCreate` hands a launcher for its child — all mint THIS. That
/// is a collapse of two functions into one, and what forced it is worth stating:
/// unit 2 gave the child's handle three bits and wrote "unit 3 revisits", design
/// 4 D-3 widened it to the self-management vocabulary plus `Start` and
/// `Transfer`, and once `Manage` was retired there was nothing left distinguishing
/// the two lists at all. A second copy of a list is a second place to forget a
/// bit.
///
/// WHAT IS IN IT, all of it named for the op it gates: `ThreadCreate`,
/// `ThreadSelf`, `Exit`, `Wait`, `EventCreate`, `WaiterCreate`, `InterruptBind`,
/// `Start`, `BootHandles`, `Give`, `Map` (M3 unit 4), `PipeCreate` (M4 unit 1),
/// `Stats` (design 16), plus the two universal bits `Transfer` and `Mint`.
///
/// TWO BITS READ ODDLY ON ROOT'S OWN HANDLE and are harmless there, which is the
/// price of one list. `Start` on a process that is already `Running` is the
/// ordinary `BadState`, and nobody starts root. `Give` into a process that is not
/// `Created` is the same refusal. Both used to be argued about per-minter; the
/// state word answers them better than a rights word could, because it answers
/// them for a CHILD too.
///
/// `InterruptBind` WAS EXPECTED TO STOP BEING HANDED OUT FREELY IN M3, AND IT
/// DID NOT — recorded here rather than left to be discovered (sawos design 9,
/// M3 unit 6's finding). The plan was §3's monotonic attenuation with `MINT_OP`
/// as the tool: root keeps the bit because it launches the drivers, and a
/// launcher spawning something that is not one hands over a handle its keep
/// mask left the bit out of. The mask cannot reach it. `SystemOp.ProcessSelf`
/// mints THIS ONE default set and takes no keep mask, so a child derives its
/// own Process handle with `InterruptBind` and `Map` on it whatever its SYSTEM
/// handle was narrowed to — a launcher's mask reaches the System sibling it
/// gives, not the Process handle the child derives from it. Withholding either
/// bit needs `ProcessSelf` to take a keep mask of its own; nothing in v1 needs
/// that (a driver child is exactly the process that should hold both), and the
/// attenuate-at-derive seam is a ruled M4 rider (`designs/010` agenda 7b).
public func process_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((ProcessRight.ThreadCreate as UInt32) | (ProcessRight.ThreadSelf as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.Exit as UInt32) | (ProcessRight.Wait as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.EventCreate as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.WaiterCreate as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.InterruptBind as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.Start as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.BootHandles as UInt32) | (ProcessRight.Give as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.Map as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.PipeCreate as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.Stats as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.Transfer as UInt32) | (ProcessRight.Mint as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a PipeInlet at `ProcessOp.PipeCreate` (spec §2.1;
/// sawos design 13).
///
/// **THE CREATOR GETS EVERY END OF ITS OWN CONNECTION**, on `event_rights`'
/// reasoning exactly: it made the object and there is nobody to attenuate
/// against yet. Attenuation happens when a handle is DERIVED for somebody else —
/// a `MINT_OP` sibling through a keep mask — and §3 makes that monotonic, so a
/// client handed a post-only inlet can never widen back to this.
///
/// `Transfer` HAS TO BE HERE, and for the reason `memory_rights` states about
/// `MapExecute`: attenuation only ever narrows, so a bit not minted at creation
/// could never appear later by any sequence of ops — and a connection whose ends
/// cannot travel is a connection with exactly one process on both sides of it,
/// which is not IPC.
public func pipe_inlet_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((PipeInletRight.Transfer as UInt32) | (PipeInletRight.Mint as UInt32)
     | (PipeInletRight.Post as UInt32) | (PipeInletRight.Wait as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a PipeOutlet at `ProcessOp.PipeCreate`. The mirror
/// of `pipe_inlet_rights`, with the verb changed and nothing else.
public func pipe_outlet_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((PipeOutletRight.Transfer as UInt32) | (PipeOutletRight.Mint as UInt32)
     | (PipeOutletRight.Take as UInt32) | (PipeOutletRight.Wait as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a PipeReply at `PipeInletOp.Post` — the CLAIM the
/// poster is handed (spec §2.1; sawos design 14 D-1).
///
/// **THE POSTER GETS EVERY BIT OF ITS OWN CLAIM**, on `pipe_inlet_rights`'
/// reasoning exactly, and `Transfer` carries the same weight it does there: §2.1
/// RATIFIES both one-shots as transferable, so a claim that could not travel
/// would be a promise the spec makes and the kernel withholds. A client that
/// wants to hand a narrower claim on mints an attenuated sibling and gives THAT.
public func pipe_reply_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((PipeReplyRight.Transfer as UInt32) | (PipeReplyRight.Mint as UInt32)
     | (PipeReplyRight.Resolve as UInt32) | (PipeReplyRight.Wait as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a PipeRequest at `PipeOutletOp.Take` — the
/// OBLIGATION the taker is handed. The mirror of `pipe_reply_rights`, with the
/// verb changed.
///
/// **`Transfer` HERE IS §2.1's DELEGATION PRIMITIVE ITSELF**, not a convenience:
/// the ratified zero-copy example is a filesystem forwarding this very handle to
/// a flash driver, which replies with the hardware bytes straight to the original
/// client. Withholding the bit would delete the example.
public func pipe_request_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((PipeRequestRight.Transfer as UInt32) | (PipeRequestRight.Mint as UInt32)
     | (PipeRequestRight.Reply as UInt32) | (PipeRequestRight.Wait as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a Memory region — a boot RAM-pool root, and every
/// region a `Split` derives from one (spec §2.5).
///
/// **ONE SET, TWO MINTERS, on `process_rights()`'s precedent** (sawos design 6
/// D-2). A boot root and a split child carry the same four bits, because a
/// split is a DERIVATION and §3 makes derivation monotonic: a caller that wants
/// a narrower child mints an attenuated sibling of it (`MINT_OP`) and hands
/// THAT on. Two default sets here would be a second place to forget a bit, and
/// would silently make "the second cut of a pool" less capable than the first.
///
/// The unit-2 docstring's promise is kept exactly: the kind-specific bits
/// are named for the ops they gate (`Split`, `Map`, `MapExecute`), per the
/// Aug-29 doctrine, rather than a generic authority.
///
/// **`MapExecute` IS IN THE SET, AND IT HAS TO BE** (sawos design 9 D-1). A boot
/// pool root and every region a `Split` derives from one carry the authority to
/// map executable, because §3's attenuation is MONOTONIC: a bit not minted at
/// boot could never appear later by any sequence of ops, so a default set that
/// withheld it would make an executable mapping unreachable in the whole system
/// rather than merely restricted. The narrowing is the HOLDER's, done with
/// `MINT_OP` and a keep mask that omits the bit — which is exactly the shape the
/// one-set-two-minters doctrine above already asks for, applied to a fourth bit.
///
/// So the default is PERMISSIVE and the policy is written where the capability
/// is handed over, not here. That is the same trade `Transfer` makes two lines
/// up: root holds everything, and what a child holds is what root chose to mint.
public func memory_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((MemoryRight.Transfer as UInt32) | (MemoryRight.Mint as UInt32)
     | (MemoryRight.Split as UInt32) | (MemoryRight.Map as UInt32)
     | (MemoryRight.MapExecute as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for an IoMemory window — a boot DEVICE-pool root, and
/// every window a `Carve` derives from one (spec §2.5; sawos design 6 D-1).
///
/// The same one-set-two-minters shape `memory_rights` has, and for the same
/// reason. `Transfer` is what makes unit 6's flow possible — root hands a
/// driver child its window — and it is minted at boot because attenuation only
/// ever narrows. **THAT FLOW RUNS NOW** (sawos design 9 D-2), which is this
/// set's bit being spent for the first time rather than merely reserved.
///
/// It gains NO `MapExecute` (`MemoryRight`'s design-9 addition): a device
/// window has no access argument to gate, so the authority would name an ask
/// nobody can make.
public func iomemory_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((IoMemoryRight.Transfer as UInt32) | (IoMemoryRight.Mint as UInt32)
     | (IoMemoryRight.Carve as UInt32) | (IoMemoryRight.Map as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a Mapping a process has just installed (spec
/// §2.5; sawos design 6 D-3).
///
/// **`Transfer` IS ABSENT BY SILENCE**, which is the one thing this set says
/// that the others do not: a Mapping names a row in one particular process's
/// domain, so it is not a capability that travels. `MappingRight` records the
/// argument. `Mint` is granted on the uniform lean the section above states —
/// a second NAME for a mapping costs nothing and can itself be attenuated,
/// which is how a holder hands out an un-unmappable reference to one.
public func mapping_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((MappingRight.Unmap as UInt32) | (MappingRight.Mint as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a thread's own handle — root's initial thread at
/// boot, and every thread `Process.ThreadCreate` makes.
///
/// `Mint` is granted on the uniform lean the default-sets section records; the
/// three kind-specific bits are the whole of the object's surface, which is deliberate — a thread's creator holds a handle
/// and the thread can mint its own through `ThreadSelf`, and both carry this
/// same set, so there is no attenuated split between the two to express.
/// (`ThreadSelf` MINTS since sawos design 3 D-4, which is what makes "both hold
/// a handle" two independently-owned instances rather than one word read twice.
/// Which bits a derived handle should withhold from a creator's is unit 3's
/// question, noted and not answered.)
public func thread_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((ThreadRight.Start as UInt32) | (ThreadRight.Join as UInt32)
     | (ThreadRight.Control as UInt32) | (ThreadRight.Mint as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for an Event a process has just created (§2.4).
///
/// The creator gets every end of its own notification — it made the object, so
/// there is nobody to attenuate against yet. Attenuation happens when a handle
/// is DERIVED for somebody else, and §3 makes that monotonic, so a producer
/// handed `Signal` alone can never widen back to this.
public func event_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((EventRight.Signal as UInt32) | (EventRight.Receive as UInt32)
     | (EventRight.Wait as UInt32) | (EventRight.Mint as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a Waiter a process has just created (§2.2).
public func waiter_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((WaiterRight.Attach as UInt32) | (WaiterRight.Wait as UInt32)
     | (WaiterRight.Mint as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for an Interrupt a process has just bound (§9).
///
/// Both ends of the cycle, for the reason `event_rights` gives: the creator
/// made the object and there is nobody to attenuate against yet. A monitor
/// handle carrying `Wait` alone is a DERIVATION, and §3 makes derivation
/// monotonic, so it can never widen back to this.
public func interrupt_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((InterruptRight.Wait as UInt32) | (InterruptRight.Ack as UInt32)
     | (InterruptRight.Mint as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a Clock a process has just been granted (§2 table).
///
/// Both ends, for the reason `event_rights` gives: the grant is the whole
/// capability and there is nobody to attenuate against yet. A child handed a
/// read-only clock is a DERIVATION, and §3 makes derivation monotonic, so it
/// can never widen back to this.
public func clock_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((ClockRight.Read as UInt32) | (ClockRight.TimerCreate as UInt32)
     | (ClockRight.Mint as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a Timer a process has just created (§2 table).
public func timer_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((TimerRight.Arm as UInt32) | (TimerRight.Wait as UInt32)
     | (TimerRight.Mint as UInt32))
}

// =============================================================================
// The UNIVERSAL ops (sawos design 3 D-2; design 4's Aug-29 rulings)
// =============================================================================
//
// TWO NOW, AND THEY SHARE ONE ARGUMENT: each is an act that is the SAME for
// every object kind, so each is one number rather than nine enum cases, and both
// are intercepted at the one point in the dispatch that knows a handle's ENTRY
// and not yet its KIND. They descend from the top of the number space so that
// the per-kind tables — dense from zero, forever — can never grow into them.

/// RELEASE — destroy the caller's handle, whatever kind it names.
///
/// **IT IS ONE NUMBER AND NOT NINE ENUM CASES**, because release is the same
/// act for every kind: unbind the entry, bump the slot's generation, touch the
/// OBJECT not at all. A per-kind case would be the same code written nine times
/// with nine ways to drift, and the dispatch would have to reach the kind
/// before it could see the op — which is one step later than the only step that
/// matters here.
///
/// **`0xFFFF` IS OUTSIDE EVERY PER-OBJECT TABLE, FOREVER, and that is a rule
/// rather than a coincidence.** Every op table in this file is DENSE FROM ZERO
/// — `SystemOp` runs 0..4, `ProcessOp` 0..8, `EventOp` 0..1 — and stays that
/// way, so no kind can ever grow into this number. A table that wanted 65535
/// ops would have to say so here first. `from(raw:)` on a per-kind enum
/// therefore never sees this value: the dispatch intercepts it BEFORE the kind
/// match, at the one point that knows the handle's ENTRY and not yet its kind.
///
/// **IT IS UNGATED** (the Aug-17 ruling, sawos design 3 D-2). No right is
/// checked, on any kind. Destroying your own capability INSTANCE harms nobody:
/// identity lives in the object's slot, not in the handle, so a released handle
/// takes nothing away from anyone else holding one. Releasing your System
/// handle or your own Process handle is legal-but-doomed — the sandboxed-compute
/// precedent — and losing the authority is what the caller asked for. If a gate
/// is ever wanted it is a NEW universal bit, never a retasking of an existing
/// one. (The Aug-17 ruling said "never a retasking of `Manage`"; the Aug-29
/// doctrine retired that bit outright, which is the same sentence taken
/// further, and the quote is kept as the history it is.)
///
/// Releasing a word that does not resolve — `NO_HANDLE`, a malformed word, a
/// stale one whose generation has moved, or a second release of the same word —
/// is the ordinary `FaultReason.BadHandle`. Using a handle you released is
/// broken code, which is the sharpened line the generations exist to draw.
///
/// CLOSE IS NOT THIS OP and is not built. Release destroys YOUR handle; close
/// would end the OBJECT for everyone, exists only on kinds with an end-state,
/// and arrives with Pipe in M4 under a per-kind right.
public static RELEASE_OP: UInt = 0xFFFF

/// MINT — produce a SIBLING handle onto the same object, in the CALLER's own
/// table, with rights the caller may only NARROW (the Aug-29 user rulings on
/// design 4).
///
/// **IT IS UNIVERSAL FOR RELEASE'S REASON.** Naming an object twice is the same
/// act for every kind — copy the entry, narrow the rights, bind the copy — so it
/// is one number the dispatch intercepts before it has decided the kind, and a
/// per-kind case would be the same code written nine times with nine ways to
/// drift. `0xFFFE` is the next number DOWN from `RELEASE_OP` for the same reason
/// that one is at the top: every per-object table is dense from zero and stays
/// below both, which the `static_assert`s below hold.
///
/// **THE ARGUMENT IS A KEEP MASK** (user ruling, Aug 29, superseding a
/// removal-mask first cut): `new = held & rights`, and all-ones is "the same
/// rights the source has", which is what the no-argument `mint()` spelling
/// means. Three things fall out of that one decision:
///
///   - **IT NEEDS NO INTROSPECTION.** There is no rights-INTROSPECTION op — a
///     process cannot ask what a handle carries — and a keep mask does not
///     require one: a caller names the rights it WANTS the sibling to have, and
///     the intersection quietly drops anything the source did not hold.
///   - **IT IS TOTAL.** Naming a right the source never had is a no-op, so there
///     is no over-ask to fault on. One policy mask therefore applies to handles
///     of several kinds without the launcher knowing which bits each carries.
///   - **NO-AMPLIFICATION IS STRUCTURAL** (§3). The result is a SUBSET of the
///     source by construction — not by a check that could be forgotten — so the
///     op cannot widen a handle even if the mask is garbage.
///
/// **THE POLARITY IS THE WHOLE RULING, AND THE REASON IS EVOLUTION: A KEEP MASK
/// FAILS CLOSED.** When a kind gains a right in some later unit, a mask written
/// before that right existed is a WHITELIST that has never heard of it and
/// therefore denies it. The removal form fails the other way — an old mask would
/// pass a new authority through silently — and the case that decided it is the
/// one this unit builds: a launcher masking a System handle for a CHILD would,
/// under a removal mask, hand every FUTURE System capability to every child it
/// had ever configured. Failing closed costs an audit when a right is added;
/// failing open costs a quiet escalation, and nobody is looking.
///
/// SUBTRACTIVE INTENT STAYS AVAILABLE AS NOTATION rather than as semantics:
/// `mint(rights: ~(Transfer as UInt32) & ALL)` is "everything except Transfer",
/// spelled so that the fail-OPEN choice is visible at the call site instead of
/// being the language of the op. A launcher that means a whitelist writes one,
/// and a launcher that means a complement writes the complement.
///
/// **IT IS GATED ON THE UNIVERSAL `Mint` BIT** (§3's bit 2, spent for this). A
/// source without it is an `AccessDenied` fault — and since `Mint` is itself a
/// bit a mask can remove, a launcher hands out a sibling that cannot mint again
/// and proliferation stops exactly where it says it does. Releasing a source
/// that does not resolve is the ordinary `BadHandle`, as for release.
///
/// The sibling is INDEPENDENT: its own table slot, its own generation, its own
/// release. It names the same object, which is what makes it a second NAME
/// rather than a second object. It took bit 1 from the retired `Manage`, whose
/// "derive children" reading half-covered this act and half-covered three
/// others — which is precisely why the Aug-29 doctrine replaced it with bits
/// named for their ops.
public static MINT_OP: UInt = 0xFFFE

// =============================================================================
// The System object's op table (spec §2)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on the System object — not a global syscall number. Another
/// object type numbers its own ops from zero, and the pair (handle, op) is what
/// selects an implementation.
///
/// Backed by `UInt`, the width of the register the op arrives in (a7), because
/// that value is PROCESS-CONTROLLED. A narrower backing would need the kernel to
/// truncate a7 before `from(raw:)` could look at it, and `0x100` would then
/// arrive as a valid `DebugPrint` — a hostile process picking an op it was
/// refused. At register width the conversion is exact and `from(raw:)` rejects
/// every number that is not an op, which is also what retired `OP_SYSTEM_MAX`:
/// the range check and the decode are now one operation instead of two that
/// could disagree.
/// `ClockGet` is the fourth, and it is a GETTER rather than a factory: the
/// object already exists — one kernel-eternal Clock per domain — so the op only
/// ever hands out a HANDLE onto it. **EVERY ASK MINTS A FRESH ONE** (sawos
/// design 3 D-4), which is the same flip `ProcessSelf` above took: a handle is a
/// capability INSTANCE, independently held and independently released, and that
/// is what an owning wrapper needs to be an owner OF. It supersedes the
/// same-handle-every-ask reading this paragraph used to carry. What §3 forbids
/// is AMPLIFICATION, not two names — a mint always carries the kind's DEFAULT
/// rights and the minting itself is gated (`ClockGet`, `ProcessSelf`) — so asking
/// twice gets a caller nothing it could not have got once. The op is
/// consequently fallible on repetition (a full handle table is `NoResource`)
/// and it earns a quota row when quotas land in unit 5.
///
/// `ProcessCreate` is the fifth, and it is THE ONE FACTORY ON THIS OBJECT —
/// placed here by the Aug-29 user ruling recorded as sawos design 2's RIDER:
/// process-scoped objects are minted by your Process; PROCESSES ARE MINTED BY
/// THE SYSTEM. A process table is machine-global and bounded by machine facts,
/// so a refusal to create one is a SYSTEM answer, while threads, events,
/// waiters and timers stay their process's own. `ProcessSelf` in this same
/// table is the precedent: a Process handle has always been something you ask
/// the System object for. It is gated on `SystemRight.ProcessCreate`; its
/// ARGUMENTS are two Memory handles, which spend no right of their own.
public enum SystemOp: UInt {
    case DebugPrint = 0,
    case Shutdown = 1,
    case ProcessSelf = 2,
    case ClockGet = 3,
    case ProcessCreate = 4,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Process object's op table (spec §2, §7, §8; design 178 D4)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on the Process object. Numbered from zero in its OWN table —
/// `ThreadCreate` is 0 here and `DebugPrint` is 0 on System, and the pair
/// (handle, op) is what tells them apart.
///
/// `ThreadCreate` lives here rather than on Thread because there is no Thread
/// to name yet, and because spec §7 puts the band map on the Process object:
/// thread creation is where a band is resolved against it, which makes the
/// Process the enforcement point. (M2 resolves nothing — D3 keeps the map a
/// loader artifact — but the op is already in the place that will.)
///
/// **`Start` IS A RECEIVER OP**, which is why it keeps the bare verb under the
/// Aug-17 naming rule: the receiver IS the process being started, so it is asked
/// of the CHILD's handle and gated on `ProcessRight.Start`. Its other half, the
/// creation that produced that child, is NOT in this table — sawos design 2's
/// RIDER (Aug 29) moved `ProcessCreate` to `SystemOp`, since a process is a
/// machine-wide resource and only the System object is machine-wide. Reading the
/// two-phase lifecycle is now reading one op on each object, which is what the
/// ruling says the phases always were.
///
/// `Start` and `BootHandleNext` RENUMBERED DOWN into the hole `ProcessCreate`
/// left, from 8/9 to 7/8. That is legal because `kernel/sysapi` is vDSO
/// discipline: an op NUMBER is not ABI, userspace names the case and this module
/// supplies the value, and the two are compiled into one image together.
///
/// `BootHandleNext` is unit 3's iterator, arriving early because root is its
/// first consumer: the boot set still enters user mode ONE handle wide (§12),
/// the register carries System, and everything else the kernel minted for root
/// at boot is DRAINED through this op one record at a time. **SINCE M3 UNIT 3
/// THE SET IT DRAINS IS PER-PROCESS** (sawos design 4 D-2): the caller's own,
/// reached through its own Process handle, whether the kernel filled it at boot
/// or a launcher filled it with `Give`.
///
/// **`Give` IS THE COURIER OP** (sawos design 4), and it keeps the bare verb
/// under the Aug-17 naming rule for the reason `Start` does: THE RECEIVER IS THE
/// CHILD. Root asks the child's Process handle to accept a capability, tagged
/// with a word root chooses; the kernel MOVES the handle into a fresh slot of
/// the child's table and answers with a STATUS ALONE, because the child-side
/// word is irrelevant to root — root can call no op through it, so nothing
/// returns it. What crosses instead is the TAG: the giver's own word handed back
/// unread at the child's drain, the `Waiter.add` key precedent exactly. The
/// kernel is a courier, never an interpreter; root and child agree on meaning
/// through config and manifest.
///
/// **`PipeCreate` IS THE FACTORY FOR A CONNECTION** (sawos design 10 ruling 3,
/// built by design 13), and it sits here rather than on System because pipes are
/// IPC and carry nothing system-shaped — the user's own words when the lean was
/// ruled. It reads `object_operation` under the Aug-17 naming rule exactly as
/// `EventCreate` and `WaiterCreate` do, and it is gated by
/// `ProcessRight.PipeCreate`.
///
/// **IT ANSWERS WITH BOTH ENDS THROUGH A COPY-OUT RECORD** — two machine words,
/// offsets published beside the wait record's — because the one-op-one-return
/// convention cannot carry two words in the value register and the copy funnel
/// is the precedented door (`Clock.Now`'s is the two-word-record precedent).
/// BOTH ends are minted into the creator's table and wired outward with `give`,
/// which is what makes a launcher able to hand the inlet to one child and the
/// outlet to another with the launch flow unchanged.
///
/// **`Stats` IS THE INTROSPECTION OP** (sawos design 16, user-ruled Sep 1), and
/// it takes the next number rather than displacing one — §5.7's renumberable-op
/// discipline says it may, and appending is what keeps every existing table row
/// where it was. It answers through a COPY-OUT record for the reason `PipeCreate`
/// and `Clock.Now` do: one op returns one word and the answer is three 64-bit
/// columns.
///
/// **THE THREE COLUMNS ARE SPLIT BY TRAP KIND BECAUSE THE SPLIT IS WHAT MAKES
/// ASSERTIONS POSSIBLE.** A single "traps" number would be the sum of a
/// deterministic quantity and a timing-dependent one, so no test could ever
/// assert it; separated, `syscalls` is exact for a given program path (a park is
/// ONE ecall however long it parks) and `interrupts` is the one that moves.
/// `faults` is exact where a test provokes one on purpose. See
/// `PROCESS_STATS_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS` for the layout and the attribution rule.
///
/// Absent, with reasons: `Kill` (see `ProcessRight`).
public enum ProcessOp: UInt {
    case ThreadCreate = 0,
    case ThreadSelf = 1,
    case Exit = 2,
    case GetStatus = 3,
    case EventCreate = 4,
    case WaiterCreate = 5,
    case InterruptBind = 6,
    case Start = 7,
    case BootHandleNext = 8,
    case Give = 9,
    case PipeCreate = 10,
    case Stats = 11,
}

/// Whether `ProcessOp.Start`'s SECOND argument carries a boot tag — the op's
/// first argument, and the thing that makes "no tag" expressible (sawos design 4
/// D-4).
///
/// **ZERO IS A LEGITIMATE TAG, so the absence cannot be encoded in the tag
/// itself.** Root's boot regions are tagged with region ordinals and ordinal 0
/// exists today, so reusing `NO_HANDLE`'s zero would have made the very first
/// row of every region table unnameable. A RESERVED ALL-ONES SENTINEL was the
/// other candidate and is refused for a sharper reason: a tag is the giver's own
/// word handed back unread, so a kernel that reserved one value would be
/// interpreting the vocabulary it exists not to interpret. A FLAG ARGUMENT costs
/// one register that `Start` was not using and reserves nothing.
///
/// It is a backed enum rather than a bare 0/1 for the house rule (design 145/153:
/// a closed named set of values is an enum, not a family of statics), which also
/// makes the decode at the trap boundary `from(raw:)` — total, so a process
/// passing 2 gets the ordinary `BadArg` fault rather than an index the kernel
/// trusts.
///
/// Backed by `UInt`, the width of the register it arrives in, for the reason
/// `EventMode` and `ClockType` give: the value is PROCESS-CONTROLLED, so a
/// narrower backing would need a truncation before `from(raw:)` and `0x100`
/// would arrive as a valid `Absent`.
///
/// The cases are `Absent`/`Present` rather than `None`/`Tag` so that nothing in
/// a `match` over one reads like an `Optional`.
///
/// **TWO CASES AND NOT THREE.** An implementation of design 4 briefly carried a
/// `Donate` form — a give performed at the start barrier — because with only one
/// Process handle per child a launcher that gave it away had nothing left to
/// `Start` with. The Aug-29 user rulings SUPERSEDED that with `MINT_OP`: a
/// launcher now mints a SIBLING of the child's Process handle, gives the
/// sibling, and starts through the original it still holds. The launch flow is
/// MINT -> GIVE -> START, three ordinary ops, and `Start` went back to doing one
/// thing.
///
/// **`Present` RESOLUTION CONSUMES THE RECORD IT NAMES** (user ruling). The
/// register IS the delivery: the tag is looked up, the word goes into the
/// child's first argument register, and that record is spent — so the drain
/// never answers with a word the child already holds. That closes a hazard the
/// first implementation had to document instead: a child that adopted a0 AND
/// took the same record's wrapper held TWO owners of ONE word, and the second
/// drop was a fault the program did nothing to deserve. It is now
/// unrepresentable.
public enum BootTagForm: UInt {
    case Absent = 0,
    case Present = 1,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Thread object's op table (spec §2, §8; design 178 D4)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on the Thread object.
///
/// `Join` is here because design 178's D4 puts it here (ratified Aug 15), which
/// REVISES spec §8's "no join syscall" (ratified Aug 3). The §8 reasoning was
/// that threads are pool workers and work completion is a task-level concept;
/// D4 keeps the object honest instead — a thread you can create and start is a
/// thread whose completion you must be able to observe, and §8 already conceded
/// the observation is needed (it proposed thread handles as WAITABLES). Join is
/// the same observation without a Waiter, which is the next unit's object. When
/// Waiter lands, `Join` becomes the one-thread shorthand for parking on it.
public enum ThreadOp: UInt {
    case Start = 0,
    case Join = 1,
    case Exit = 2,
    case Yield = 3,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Event object's op table (spec §2.4; design 178 D5)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on an Event object.
///
/// TWO OPS AND THE OBJECT IS COMPLETE, which is why §2.4 calls it the smallest
/// thing that can be a kernel object at all: bits go IN and never block, and one
/// read takes them all OUT and resets. There is no third question to ask an
/// Event — "is it ready" is `word != 0`, and it is asked by the Waiter, not by
/// an op.
///
/// `Receive` IS THE NON-BLOCKING DOOR, not the only one: a `Waiter.wait`
/// delivery takes the same word the same way (ruled Aug 17, user). What the two
/// differ in is parking, which is why this stayed one op rather than growing a
/// blocking twin.
///
/// There is deliberately no non-draining PEEK. A drain that could be skipped
/// would make level-triggered readiness a thing a reader could observe without
/// clearing, so a poll loop could spin forever on a readiness nothing consumes;
/// §2.4's "one drain reads-and-clears" is what forecloses that, and one op is
/// how it stays foreclosed.
public enum EventOp: UInt {
    case Signal = 0,
    case Receive = 1,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Waiter object's op table (spec §2.2; design 178 D5)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on a Waiter object.
///
/// The names are §2.2's own (`add`, `remove`, `wait`); the RIGHT that gates the
/// first two is `WaiterRight.Attach`, which is one capability under a name that
/// says what it is rather than repeating either op.
///
/// **`Remove` TAKES THE KEY, NOT THE WAITABLE'S HANDLE** (ruled Aug 16, user).
/// Detaching edits the WAITER's own attachment table and never touches the
/// waitable at all, so the authority it needs is the Waiter handle already in
/// the handle register plus the key that names the attachment — asking for the
/// waitable's handle too would be asking for authority the operation does not
/// spend. It also has to be this way for M3: once handles can be CLOSED, a
/// closed waitable's stale attachment is one whose handle no longer resolves,
/// and a handle-based remove could never name it.
///
/// THE INVARIANT THAT FORCES: **keys are UNIQUE per Waiter.** `Add` with a key
/// the Waiter already uses is a FAULT — caller-checkable, since the attacher
/// chose both keys — and it has to be refused rather than allowed, because a
/// duplicate makes two different questions ambiguous at once: which attachment
/// a `Remove` names, and which one a `Wait` answer came from.
public enum WaiterOp: UInt {
    case Add = 0,
    case Remove = 1,
    case Wait = 2,
}

/// **`Add`'s THIRD ARGUMENT: THE ATTACHMENT FLAGS** (design 10 rulings 9(b) and
/// 11(c); sawos design 15, M4 unit 3).
///
/// TWO BITS, AND THEY ARE ORTHOGONAL AXES rather than three modes. Bit 0 says
/// what happens AT DELIVERY (detach, or stay subscribed); bit 1 says what
/// happens to the caller's HANDLE ENTRY at the attach (the op releases it, and
/// the attachment owns the object from then on). A 2x2, which is exactly why
/// ruling 11(c) struck `OneShotConsuming` as an enum case.
///
/// **THE TYPED SURFACE SPLITS THEM DIFFERENTLY, AND ON PURPOSE.** Consuming is
/// an EFFECT change — the wrapper is taken BY MOVE and is provably dead at the
/// call site — and ruling 4's own mode-enum rejection applies: a runtime flag
/// can change neither a return type nor a Saw function's effect. So `sos` spells
/// the consuming attach as a distinct verb (`Waiter.give`) while `AttachMode`
/// stays a defaulted value parameter. Here, at the wire, both are bits: the
/// kernel's job is one op, and which of the four combinations it was told is a
/// number.
///
/// A word with any OTHER bit set is a `BadArg` fault — the set is published, so
/// the caller could have checked, which is §5.7's own line.
public static WAITER_ADD_ONESHOT: UInt = 1 << 0
public static WAITER_ADD_CONSUMING: UInt = 1 << 1

/// Every flag `Add` knows. Anything outside it is outside the op's domain.
public static WAITER_ADD_FLAGS: UInt = WAITER_ADD_ONESHOT | WAITER_ADD_CONSUMING

// =============================================================================
// The Interrupt object's op table (spec §2 table, §9; design 178 D6)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on an Interrupt object.
///
/// ONE OP, and the object is complete — which is the same claim `EventOp` makes
/// and for a sharper reason. Everything an Interrupt does TO a process happens
/// through a Waiter (§2.2), and everything the KERNEL does to the line happens
/// in the trap path; what is left for the holder to ask is the one question
/// spec §9 names: this fire is finished, let the device interrupt again.
///
/// There is deliberately no `Wait` op here. Parking is the Waiter's, and a
/// second way to park on one Interrupt would be a second wake protocol to keep
/// in step with the first — §2.2's aggregator exists precisely so that every
/// waitable has exactly one.
///
/// There is deliberately no `Bind`/`Unbind` pair either: the binding IS the
/// object's existence (`ProcessOp.InterruptBind` takes the line), so a bound
/// line is a live slot and an unbound one is a slot that was freed. A rebindable
/// Interrupt would let one handle name two different devices over its life,
/// which is exactly what a capability must not do.
public enum InterruptOp: UInt {
    case Ack = 0,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Clock object's op table (spec §2 table; design 232 unit 1)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on a Clock object.
///
/// TWO OPS: read the time, and make something that fires against it. There is
/// deliberately no SET — a Monotonic clock that could be set would not be one,
/// and the domains that CAN step (Realtime) are future values of `ClockType`
/// that will bring their own op if they need it.
///
/// `Now` answers through a COPY-OUT RECORD rather than the value register,
/// because a nanosecond count is 64 bits and one of the two profiles has 32-bit
/// registers. The vDSO true-mapping upgrade (spec §5.7) is the named
/// syscall-free future for this one op and changes nothing about its shape.
public enum ClockOp: UInt {
    case Now = 0,
    case TimerCreate = 1,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Timer object's op table (spec §2 table; design 232 unit 1)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on a Timer object.
///
/// TWO OPS, AND NO ACK — which is the difference from an Interrupt worth reading
/// here. §9's cycle needs an ack because the KERNEL masks the line and only the
/// driver can say the device has been serviced; a Timer has no device and no
/// mask, so it re-arms itself and a fire is consumed by the WAIT that reports
/// it. That is the timerfd model, and it is what makes a periodic timer a
/// fire-and-forget schedule rather than a request/response.
///
/// `Arm` takes its arguments through a COPY-IN RECORD, because two 64-bit
/// nanosecond counts are four words on a 32-bit profile and the argument
/// registers are three. See `TIMER_ARM_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS` below.
///
/// `Disarm` is separate from "arm with no deadline" because the two mean
/// different things to the fire count: arming REPLACES a schedule and clears
/// what the old one owed, while disarming says the timer is off and owes
/// nothing at all. Collapsing them would make "cancel" and "reschedule"
/// indistinguishable at the ABI.
public enum TimerOp: UInt {
    case Arm = 0,
    case Disarm = 1,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Memory object's op table (spec §2.5; sawos design 6 D-2, D-3)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on a Memory object.
///
/// **TWO OPS: DERIVE, AND INSTALL.** They are the whole of what a RAM region is
/// for, and neither reads it — there is still no op that hands back a Memory's
/// bounds, so a region stays a CAPABILITY rather than a description (spec
/// §2.5's slice-1 property, unchanged by growing a table).
///
/// **`Split` IS ONE CUT FROM THE FRONT** (design 6 D-2): `split(len)` mints a
/// new Memory over `[base, base + len)` and THE PARENT BECOMES THE REMAINDER
/// `[base + len, top)`. Allocation is repeated front-splits, so THE PARENT IS
/// THE POOL CURSOR and a pool needs no second bookkeeping — every piece stays
/// representable in the one `{base, len}` slot shape the object already has.
///
/// ARBITRARY-OFFSET CARVING IS REFUSED BY THE SHAPE, NOT BY A CHECK, and that
/// is worth recording where the op is: a cut from the middle would fragment the
/// parent into TWO remainders, and one slot cannot hold two ranges. So the
/// restriction is not a rule somebody could relax without noticing — it is what
/// the representation permits.
///
/// It is exclusive, which is the difference from `IoMemoryOp.Carve`: what a
/// split hands out, the parent no longer has. Free-on-last-reference (§2.5's
/// refcount clause) is UNIT 5's, so a split is permanent until teardown and
/// teardown returns SLOTS rather than ranges. Stated, not hidden.
///
/// **`Map` INSTALLS THE REGION IN A DOMAIN** and answers with a Mapping handle
/// to the caller. Its arguments are a target Process handle and the access bits
/// (`MapAccess`), and it spends TWO rights on two objects — `MemoryRight.Map`
/// here and `ProcessRight.Map` on the target — because possession of bytes must
/// not imply authority over an address space.
///
/// **AND A THIRD, CONDITIONALLY** (sawos design 9 D-1): an access word naming
/// `MapAccess.Execute` also spends `MemoryRight.MapExecute` on this region.
/// Conditional because it is a bit about WHAT is being asked for rather than
/// about the op — a caller mapping RW spends exactly what it always did.
public enum MemoryOp: UInt {
    case Split = 0,
    case Map = 1,
}

// =============================================================================
// The IoMemory object's op table (spec §2.5; sawos design 6 D-1, D-2, D-3)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on an IoMemory object.
///
/// The same two shapes `MemoryOp` has, with the derivation verb changed — and
/// the change is the kind's whole reason for existing.
///
/// **`Carve` LEAVES THE PARENT ALONE** (design 6 D-2): `carve(offset, len)`
/// mints a sub-window over `[base + offset, base + offset + len)` and the
/// parent is UNCHANGED. That is §2.5's "a fixed region may be handed out many
/// times" — MMIO is pinned, so there is nothing to consume and several drivers
/// may legitimately hold windows onto one peripheral.
///
/// **THE HARDWARE'S OWN GRANULARITY IS CHECKED AT THE CARVE**, against a
/// per-profile HAL predicate, and violating it is a `BadArg` FAULT because the
/// caller chose both numbers. One profile expresses a device window as a single
/// naturally-aligned power-of-two protection entry, so `len` must be a power of
/// two and the base a multiple of it; the other grants by page.
///
/// **CHECKING IT AT THE CARVE IS THE POINT, AND `Map` CHECKS IT AGAIN.** At the
/// carve because that is where a caller chose the numbers, and a window that
/// could never be installed anywhere is a capability that lies about itself —
/// a refusal deferred to the map would arrive one op later than the mistake.
/// At the map because of the ONE window a carve never saw: a boot ROOT, whose
/// bounds came from the build's region table rather than from any caller. Two
/// checks, two different things being distrusted.
///
/// **`Map` INSTALLS A DEVICE-ATTRIBUTE ROW, always**, which is the type-level
/// guarantee `ObjType.IoMemory` exists for: there is no other op on this kind,
/// so a cacheable view of a register block is unreachable by construction.
/// Access is RW and fixed — execute-on-device is already refused vocabulary
/// (registers are not instructions), and a write-only register block is not a
/// grant either profile can express.
public enum IoMemoryOp: UInt {
    case Carve = 0,
    case Map = 1,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Mapping object's op table (spec §2.5; sawos design 6 D-3)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on a Mapping object.
///
/// ONE OP, and the object is complete — the same claim `InterruptOp` makes.
/// §2.5 gives a Mapping exactly one job: it is the thing that can UNMAP, and
/// everything else about it (where the row is, what access it carries) was
/// decided when it was installed and cannot be edited afterwards. A mapping
/// whose access should change is a mapping that is unmapped and made again,
/// which is one fewer op and no ambiguity about what a half-changed row means.
///
/// **UNMAP OF AN ALREADY-UNMAPPED MAPPING IS `BadState`** — caller-checkable,
/// since the caller is the one that unmapped it. The same tag covers a mapping
/// whose TARGET PROCESS has died: its row went with the domain, so there is
/// nothing left to remove, and "this object is not in a state to do that" is
/// exactly what happened.
public enum MappingOp: UInt {
    case Unmap = 0,
}

// =============================================================================
// The PipeInlet and PipeOutlet op tables (spec §2.1; sawos design 13)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on a PipeInlet — the CLIENT end.
///
/// **ONE OP IN UNIT 1, AND RULING 4 IS WHY.** `post` IS the one kernel primitive
/// on the client side: stage the message, never suspend, answer. Blocking send,
/// `send(timeout:)`, TELL and cancel are LIBRARY COMPOSITIONS over it plus the
/// Timer and Waiter machinery that already shipped — so the kernel never learns
/// what a timeout is, and there is no second submission door for this table to
/// hold.
///
/// **IT ANSWERS THE CLAIM SINCE M4 UNIT 2** (sawos design 14), which is exactly
/// what unit 1 said at this table would happen: the value register carries a
/// `PipeReply` handle minted into the poster's table, and an op growing a return
/// between units cost nothing under §5.7's renumberable-op discipline —
/// userspace names the case, this module supplies the number, the two compile
/// together. A REFUSED post mints nothing, so `SosStatus.WouldBlock` and
/// `SosStatus.PeerClosed` leave the value register meaningless and the typed tier
/// answers `Ok(None)` for the first.
///
/// ITS ARGUMENTS ARE (address, length), taken through the copy-IN funnel
/// `Timer.Arm` opened: a body up to `PIPE_BODY_BYTES`, and a length of ZERO is
/// legal because §2.1 makes a data-free message an option.
/// **AND A SECOND OP SINCE M4 UNIT 3.5** (sawos design 17, on design 10 ruling
/// 4's Aug-31 rider): `Call` is `post`, the park and the resolve run WITHOUT
/// RETURNING TO USERSPACE. It is a performance amendment and not a semantic
/// one — every rule the composed path obeys applies verbatim — so the two ops
/// sit in one table rather than one replacing the other: `post` is still the
/// primitive a multiplexer builds on, and `Call` is the fused shape of the
/// blocking RPC that dominates.
public enum PipeInletOp: UInt {
    case Post = 0,
    /// **POST, PARK, AND RESOLVE BEHIND ONE TRAP** (design 10 ruling 4's rider;
    /// sawos design 17).
    ///
    /// Its arguments are (body, length, reply buffer) and its value is the
    /// REPLY's length. It is gated by `PipeInletRight.Post` and by nothing else
    /// — a `Call` submits a message exactly as a `post` does, and the fusion is
    /// mechanics rather than authority. The park needs no `Wait` right because
    /// no Waiter is involved: the claim is KERNEL-INTERNAL, tied to the parked
    /// thread, consumed at the reply, so a fused call mints no `PipeReply` into
    /// the caller's table and spends no handle row.
    ///
    /// **IT NEVER ANSWERS `WouldBlock`** (ruling 8's level, reached from
    /// inside): a full ring parks on the inlet's room-to-post level exactly as
    /// the composed send does, and the poster resumes when an exchange settles.
    /// `PeerClosed` is the one refusal, before or during, and it is what a
    /// dropped obligation, a dead server end and an abandoned claim all come
    /// back as.
    ///
    /// **NO DURATION ARGUMENT**: the kernel still never learns what a timeout
    /// is (ruling 4). A caller that wants one composes `post` + `wait` + a
    /// Timer, exactly as before.
    ///
    /// **THE REPLY BUFFER CARRIES NO CAPACITY**, on `PipeOutletOp.Take`'s own
    /// precedent (design 14 deviation 1): the third argument register is spent
    /// on the buffer's ADDRESS, so the buffer is checked against
    /// `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` through the copy door and against the caller's REAL
    /// memory — a stronger test than a number the caller typed.
    case Call = 1,
}

/// A METHOD ID on a PipeOutlet — the SERVER end.
///
/// **ONE OP, AND IT IS NONBLOCKING.** `take` copies the OLDEST staged message
/// out through the funnel and answers its length; an empty ring is
/// `SosStatus.WouldBlock`, never a park, because unit 1 has no waitability and
/// ruling 4's ladder has the proofs POLL until unit 3 lands it.
///
/// **IT ANSWERS TWO THINGS SINCE M4 UNIT 2** (sawos design 14): the message, and
/// the `PipeRequest` that obliges its reply — minted into the TAKER's table at
/// the take. One op answers one word, so the two travel in a copy-out RECORD
/// (`PIPE_TAKE_RECORD_WORDS` below), exactly as `ProcessOp.PipeCreate`'s two ends
/// do; growing an op's answer between units is what §5.7's renumberable-op
/// discipline makes free, and unit 1 said at this very table that it would.
///
/// Its arguments are (record, record capacity, body). The BODY carries no
/// capacity of its own any more: it is checked against `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` —
/// still the published minimum — through the copy door and against the caller's
/// REAL memory, which is a stronger test than the number the caller used to type.
public enum PipeOutletOp: UInt {
    case Take = 0,
}

/// A METHOD ID on a PipeReply — the CLIENT's claim on one reply (spec §2.1;
/// sawos design 14).
///
/// **ONE OP, NONBLOCKING, AND IT CONSUMES.** `resolve` answers the reply bytes if
/// they are there — consuming the claim's entry with them — `SosStatus.WouldBlock`
/// while the exchange is still pending, and `SosStatus.PeerClosed` when the server
/// side abandoned it. **THE BLOCKING `send(msg)` / `send(msg, timeout:)`
/// COMPOSITIONS ARE BUILT ON TOP OF IT AND DO NOT USE IT** (M4 unit 3, sawos
/// design 15): ruling 11(a) put the reply in the WAIT RECORD, so a client that
/// attaches its claim never resolves at all, and this op is the polling door
/// that remains for a caller that chose it. A claim whose answer was delivered
/// through a wait answers `PeerClosed` here — there is no second copy.
///
/// Its arguments are (buffer, capacity) and its value is the reply's LENGTH —
/// `PipeOutletOp.Take`'s unit-1 shape, reused verbatim on the way back.
public enum PipeReplyOp: UInt {
    case Resolve = 0,
}

/// A METHOD ID on a PipeRequest — the SERVER's obligation (spec §2.1; sawos
/// design 14).
///
/// **ONE OP, AND IT DISCHARGES THE OBLIGATION EITHER WAY.** `reply` copies the
/// reply body in and CONSUMES the request. To an ABANDONED request — the client
/// dropped its claim — it answers `SosStatus.PeerClosed` and the obligation is
/// discharged all the same, which is §2.1's ratified sentence: never a silent
/// success, ignorable with `let _ =` on the std-Channel close precedent.
///
/// Its arguments are (body, length), `PipeInletOp.Post`'s shape mirrored — a
/// length of ZERO is legal here too, because a reply that carries no data is
/// still an answer.
/// **AND A SECOND OP SINCE M4 UNIT 3.5** (sawos design 17, on design 10 ruling
/// 4's rider as amended Sep 1): `ReplyWait` is a reply with a BUILT-IN WAIT —
/// the server's half of the fused pair, and the mirror of `PipeInletOp.Call`.
///
/// **IT LIVES ON THE REQUEST, WHICH IS THE USER'S OWN PLACEMENT** (Sep 1): the
/// reply is the primary act and it is what consumes the receiver, so the op
/// belongs to the object being consumed and the wait is the fused tail. There
/// is NO degenerate form and NO optional waiter — the first turn of a server
/// loop is the existing `WaiterOp.Wait`, the dead-client recovery is that same
/// call, and reply-without-wait is `Reply` above. An optional waiter would
/// change the return type on a runtime value, which is the exact shape ruling 4
/// and ruling 11(c) both refused.
public enum PipeRequestOp: UInt {
    case Reply = 0,
    /// **DISCHARGE THIS OBLIGATION, THEN PARK ON A WAITER, BEHIND ONE TRAP**
    /// (sawos design 17).
    ///
    /// Its arguments are (args record, args record bytes, wait record buffer)
    /// and it is gated by `PipeRequestRight.Reply` here PLUS the waiter's own
    /// `WaiterRight.Wait` — both checked, because the op does both things. The
    /// args record is `PIPE_REPLY_WAIT_RECORD_WORDS` long and carries the
    /// waiter handle, the reply body's address and its length; the reply body
    /// could not ride the registers beside a wait buffer, so the SMALL words
    /// travel through the copy-IN funnel `TimerOp.Arm` opened and the body
    /// stays an address, which is what keeps the wrapper from copying 128 bytes
    /// of its own.
    ///
    /// **THE REQUEST IS CONSUMED ON EVERY PATH** (§2.1's ratified sentence, and
    /// the transfer-funnel contract): a refused reply discharges the obligation
    /// exactly as `Reply` does, and the op then returns WITHOUT PARKING so that
    /// one dead client can never stall a server loop.
    ///
    /// **THE ANSWER HAS TWO CHANNELS AND THE VALUE REGISTER IS THE LEG**
    /// (`ReplyWaitLeg`): `Ok` is a delivered wait record, and a failure carries
    /// the status plus which half of the op produced it. `Wait` is ZERO for the
    /// reason that makes the encoding free — every outcome after the park is a
    /// wait-leg outcome, and every waker in the kernel already writes a zero
    /// value register — so only the reply leg has to say anything at all.
    case ReplyWait = 1,
}

/// **WHICH HALF OF A `PipeRequestOp.ReplyWait` PRODUCED THE ANSWER** (sawos
/// design 17; user-ruled Sep 1 as the leg-tagged error).
///
/// It rides the VALUE register beside the status, and `Wait = 0` is
/// load-bearing rather than arbitrary: a `ReplyWait` that gets past its reply
/// leg PARKS, and every path that answers a parked thread — a delivery, a
/// revocation, a teardown — writes a zero value register already. So the wait
/// leg needs no cooperation from any waker, and the reply leg, which is the
/// only outcome that returns without parking, is the one that stamps a number.
///
/// `Wait(_)` therefore says THE REPLY LANDED, which is the distinction the
/// server loop is written against: a `Reply` leg failure means recover by
/// waiting afresh, and a `Wait` leg failure means the exchange is complete and
/// the WAITER is what broke.
public enum ReplyWaitLeg: UInt {
    case Wait = 0,
    case Reply = 1,
}

// EVERY OP TABLE IS DENSE FROM ZERO, AND STAYS BELOW THE UNIVERSAL OPS (design
// 3 D-2, widened by design 4's `MINT_OP`). That is what makes a universal op
// number safe: it can never collide with a per-kind op, because no kind is
// allowed to reach it. The bound tested is `MINT_OP`, the LOWER of the two, so
// one assert per table covers both and adding a third universal op below it
// tightens every one of these at once. One assert per table, on its highest
// case, in the spirit of the rights asserts above — the repetition IS the check,
// and a table growing toward the top of the space fails to build rather than
// failing as a release or a mint that quietly ran somebody's op.
static_assert((SystemOp.ProcessCreate as UInt) < MINT_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((ProcessOp.Stats as UInt) < MINT_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((ThreadOp.Yield as UInt) < MINT_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((EventOp.Receive as UInt) < MINT_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((WaiterOp.Wait as UInt) < MINT_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((InterruptOp.Ack as UInt) < MINT_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((ClockOp.TimerCreate as UInt) < MINT_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((TimerOp.Disarm as UInt) < MINT_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((MemoryOp.Map as UInt) < MINT_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((IoMemoryOp.Map as UInt) < MINT_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((MappingOp.Unmap as UInt) < MINT_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((PipeInletOp.Call as UInt) < MINT_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((PipeOutletOp.Take as UInt) < MINT_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((PipeReplyOp.Resolve as UInt) < MINT_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((PipeRequestOp.ReplyWait as UInt) < MINT_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")

// ...and the two universal numbers are distinct and ordered, which is the fact
// the eight asserts above lean on when they test the lower one.
static_assert(MINT_OP < RELEASE_OP,
              "the universal ops descend from the top of the number space")

/// WHICH CLOCK a Timer runs against, and what `System.ClockGet` names (§2's
/// Clock row; design 232 unit 1, the M3 time notes).
///
/// **v1 DECLARES `Monotonic` AND NOTHING ELSE.** `Boot` and `Realtime` are named
/// in the design as future domains and are deliberately NOT declared here: an
/// unproducible case is dead surface, and a caller able to name one the kernel
/// cannot mint would get a runtime refusal where the absence is a compile error.
/// The raw backing is what lets them be ADDED without renumbering (design 145's
/// wire idiom), which is the whole point of stating the number.
///
/// Backed by `UInt`, the width of the register the domain arrives in, for the
/// reason `SystemOp` and `EventMode` give: the value is PROCESS-CONTROLLED, so a
/// narrower backing would need a truncation before `from(raw:)` and `0x100`
/// would arrive as a valid `Monotonic`.
///
/// MONOTONIC'S CONTRACT: nanoseconds since the machine started counting, never
/// stepping and never going backwards. The epoch is not published and must not
/// be relied on — two readings are comparable, one reading means nothing on its
/// own.
public enum ClockType: UInt {
    case Monotonic = 0,
}

/// WHAT A MAPPING PERMITS — the access bits `MemoryOp.Map` takes (spec §2.5's
/// `map(access:)`; sawos design 6 D-3).
///
/// **ACCESS IS A PROPERTY OF THE MAPPING, NOT OF THE REGION**, and this enum is
/// where that decision becomes API. `MemoryRight` deliberately has no R/W/X
/// pair (its own docstring says why), so the only place a process names what it
/// may do with memory is here — which is exactly what makes DOUBLE-MAPPING one
/// region read-only in one place and read-write in another expressible. Agenda
/// item 7's lean lands with it: a double map is ALLOWED, two rows and two
/// Mappings, with no aliasing bookkeeping owed on a machine that does not
/// translate.
///
/// **IT IS A FLAG SET, so the values are BITS and a combination is the BACKING
/// INTEGER** — Saw ships no OptionSet and an enum is a set of tags (design
/// 185's flag-enum rule). `MapAccess.Read | MapAccess.Write` folds to `3` in
/// any fixed-width slot, which is what the funnels take.
///
/// **THE NUMBERS ARE THE `SegFlag` NUMBERS, and that is a fact rather than a
/// coincidence**: R = 1, W = 2, X = 4 is the order one profile's protection
/// config uses natively, so an image's declared permissions and a caller's
/// requested access are the same vocabulary the whole way down. The kernel
/// still TRANSLATES rather than casting — one small function beside the map op
/// — because the two enums live in two packages that must be free to version
/// apart.
///
/// WHAT IS REFUSED, and all three are caller-checkable `BadArg` faults: write
/// without read (not a grant any target should install, and one profile
/// RESERVES the encoding outright), no access at all (a row that permits
/// nothing is a row that spends a protection slot to say no, which default-deny
/// already says), and — since sawos design 9 D-1 — **WRITE AND EXECUTE IN ONE
/// ROW**.
///
/// **W^X IS PER ROW, AND IT COSTS NOTHING EXPRESSIBLE.** Double-mapping is
/// already sanctioned (design 6's agenda item 7: two rows, two Mappings, no
/// aliasing bookkeeping owed on a machine that does not translate), so the
/// JIT-shaped pattern — an RW row here, an RX row there, over one region —
/// remains available in full while every individual protection row is W^X. What
/// the refusal removes is the ONE shape that has no honest use: a single row a
/// process can write to and then fetch from.
///
/// It joins write-without-read rather than living beside it as a different kind
/// of check, because they are the same kind: both are grants the caller
/// assembled out of cases it named, and both are answered before anything is
/// installed.
///
/// **THE BOUNDARY IS THE DYNAMIC MAP, STATED SO IT IS NOT MISREAD.** Neither
/// this rule nor `MemoryRight.MapExecute` governs a process image's OWN
/// executable segments: those arrive through `process_create`'s loader as
/// `SegFlag` bits read out of the image, under `SystemRight.ProcessCreate`
/// authority, and are validated by `imgformat.has_sane_perms` (which refuses
/// write-without-read and X-on-device, and deliberately not W|X — an image's
/// linker script is what keeps its segments apart). A driver child holds no
/// `ProcessCreate`, so the two doors are not alternatives for it.
///
/// Backed by `UInt32` rather than by the register's `UInt`, which is the one
/// place this file's usual reasoning inverts and is worth stating: a MASK is
/// not a decoded number. It never reaches `from(raw:)` — the kernel tests bits
/// and refuses anything outside the three — so there is no truncation hazard to
/// avoid, and a fixed-width backing is what makes `MapAccess.Read |
/// MapAccess.Write` fold at an ordinary annotated slot (DF-240a's adoption
/// reaches fixed-width slots and not platform ones). `SystemRight`'s keep mask
/// is the precedent: both are bit sets a caller assembles.
public enum MapAccess: UInt32 {
    case Read = 1 << 0,
    case Write = 1 << 1,
    case Execute = 1 << 2,
}

/// Every bit `MapAccess` defines — what a caller's access word may contain.
///
/// Named rather than written so the kernel's "these bits and no others" check
/// and this declaration cannot drift: adding a case here is what widens the
/// check.
public static MAP_ACCESS_MASK: UInt32 = ((MapAccess.Read as UInt32)
                                         | (MapAccess.Write as UInt32)
                                         | (MapAccess.Execute as UInt32))

/// How an Event ACCUMULATES — a per-Event property, chosen at creation and
/// fixed for its life (§2.4).
///
/// It is fixed rather than settable because the accumulated word means two
/// different things under the two modes, and a mode change with a non-zero word
/// would have to either reinterpret it or discard it. Creating a second Event
/// costs one slab slot.
///
/// Backed by `UInt`, the width of the register the mode arrives in, for the
/// reason `SystemOp` gives: the value is PROCESS-CONTROLLED, so a narrower
/// backing would need a truncation before `from(raw:)` and `0x100` would arrive
/// as a valid `Or`.
public enum EventMode: UInt {
    case Or = 0,
    case SaturatingSum = 1,
}

/// **THE MESSAGE BODY'S MAXIMUM, AS A BUILD DEFINE** (spec §2.1's fixed body;
/// sawos design 10 ruling 6).
///
/// It is the size of one staging slot, the largest `len` a `PipeInletOp.Post`
/// may name, the smallest buffer a `PipeOutletOp.Take` may be handed, and —
/// since M4 unit 3 — the size of the BODY REGION every wait record buffer
/// carries. All four are one number because they are one fixed slot seen from
/// four sides.
///
/// §2.1 ratifies a FIXED body maximum without fixing the number, so this is the
/// tree's choice within it: large enough for a protocol header plus a small
/// payload, small enough that `PIPE_INFLIGHT` slots per connection stay a
/// zerofill array rather than a budget anybody has to think about. The assert
/// below pins the range the ratified sentence implies — a body must hold at
/// least a machine word, and must stay small enough that one bounded copy is
/// still a bounded copy (see design 1's preemption-point audit).
///
/// **IT IS DECLARED HERE, ABOVE THE WAIT RECORD, BECAUSE THE RECORD NAMES IT**
/// (sawos design 15, on design 10 ruling 11(a)). A statics initializer folds in
/// DECLARATION ORDER, so a size an array length reads has to be above it; the
/// number used to sit with the pipe records at the bottom of this file, and the
/// pointer comment there is what is left of that placement.
public static PIPE_BODY_BYTES: Int = 128

static_assert(PIPE_BODY_BYTES >= 8,
              "a message body must hold at least one 64-bit word")
static_assert(PIPE_BODY_BYTES <= 1024,
              "a message body is copied with interrupts masked — keep it bounded")
static_assert(PIPE_BODY_BYTES % 8 == 0,
              "a staging slot must be doubleword-sized on both profiles")

// =============================================================================
// The wait record (spec §2.2, as amended by design 178 M2 unit 3 rider 3, and
// grown a BODY by design 10 ruling 11(a) — sawos design 15, M4 unit 3)
// =============================================================================
//
// `Waiter.Wait` answers with a RECORD the kernel COPIES OUT into a buffer the
// caller supplies, not with registers. That is what lets the answer carry a
// per-waitable payload without the ABI growing a register per object kind: a
// Pipe's readiness is not a Timer's is not an Event's, and a fixed register
// pair would have had to be the union of all of them forever.
//
// THE KEY IS UNIVERSAL AND IS FACTORED OUT. Every waitable answers with the
// word its attacher supplied (§2.2: it can directly encode the waiting task's
// identity, so the kernel neither interprets it nor constrains it); only the
// PAYLOAD varies by kind. So the record is `key, tag, payload...` and a reader
// gets the key without knowing what fired.
//
// THE WIRE FORMAT IS PUBLIC AND FIXED-WIDTH-BY-WORD: word 0 the key, word 1 the
// tag, word 2 onward the payload. It is machine words rather than fixed 32-bit
// fields because it never leaves the machine — unlike `sosimg`, which is a file
// two profiles must both read (design 47's discipline applies there and not
// here), this crosses one privilege boundary inside one address space.

/// Which kind of waitable a record describes — the record's TAG WORD.
///
/// Raw-backed (design 145's wire idiom): every case states its number, so a
/// reorder cannot renumber them, and the userspace decode reads it through
/// `from(raw:)`, which is total. The tag SPACE is extensible: §2.2 and §8
/// already name the kinds that follow (a Pipe readable or reply-ready, a
/// Timer fired, an Interrupt asserted, a Thread or Process exited), and each
/// takes the next number without moving this one.
///
/// AN UNKNOWN TAG IS A PANIC in the decode, not a `None` a caller handles. The
/// kernel WROTE it, out of this same declaration — so a tag userspace cannot
/// name is a kernel/`sos` skew, which is a bug in the pair rather than data
/// from an untrusted source. Never-hide says it should be loud.
///
/// **THE EXTENSIBILITY PARAGRAPH ABOVE WAS COLLECTED BY `Process = 3`** (sawos
/// design 8, M3 unit 5.5): the fourth tag took the next number, moved none of
/// the three, and broke every exhaustive `WaitPayload` match in the tree until
/// each said what it does about a death — which is the raw backing and the
/// exhaustive match doing exactly what this docstring promised they would.
/// **AND COLLECTED FOUR MORE TIMES BY M4 UNIT 3** (sawos design 15, on design 10
/// rulings 8 and 11(a)): the four pipe arms took the next four numbers, moved
/// none of the four above them, and broke every exhaustive `WaitPayload` match
/// in the tree until each said what it does about a pipe.
public enum WaitTag: UInt {
    case Event = 0,
    case Interrupt = 1,
    case Timer = 2,
    /// A PROCESS ENDED, any cause (spec §8; sawos design 8). The payload is the
    /// §8 status word — `process_status_word(kind, code)` — so the `Exited` /
    /// `Faulted` distinction rides vocabulary that already shipped rather than
    /// a second tag per ending.
    case Process = 3,
    /// **A PipeOutlet IS READABLE** — the server end's level (spec §2.2's
    /// waitable list; sawos design 15). The payload is an `OutletLevel`: `Msg`
    /// while anything is staged, `PeerGone` when the ring is dry AND every
    /// inlet handle is gone. Drain-then-terminal, so `Msg` wins while there is
    /// anything left to hand over (design 230's close-drains-first rule).
    case Readable = 4,
    /// **A PipeInlet HAS ROOM TO POST** — the client end's level (design 10
    /// ruling 8). The payload is an `InletLevel`: `Room` while the staging ring
    /// has a free slot, `PeerGone` when every outlet handle is gone. A plain
    /// level, not a terminal one — consume clears nothing, and the level's end
    /// is the poster's own next post.
    case Room = 5,
    /// **A PipeReply's ANSWER HAS ARRIVED** — and this is the tag that made the
    /// record grow (design 10 ruling 11(a)). The payload is a `ReplyLevel`, and
    /// a `Data` delivery carries the reply BYTES in the record's body region:
    /// the wait itself is the delivery, so the composed client never calls
    /// `resolve` on the success path.
    case Reply = 6,
    /// **A PipeRequest's CLAIM WAS ABANDONED** — the server's cancellation
    /// watcher (spec §2.1's "the `PipeRequestHandle` is WAITABLE for
    /// abandoned"). No payload: the fact IS the notification, and it is
    /// TERMINAL, because a reply column that reached zero can never rise.
    case Abandoned = 7,
}

/// Words in a wait record's HEADER: key, tag, payload, body length.
///
/// **THE FOURTH WORD IS M4 UNIT 3's** (design 10 ruling 11(a)). A delivery may
/// now carry BYTES — a reply body, up to `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` of it — and the
/// header word is how many of the body region are real. It is zero for every
/// word-payload kind, which is all of them but one.
public static WAIT_HEADER_WORDS: Int = 4

/// Where each lives. Named rather than written, on both sides of the boundary.
public static WAIT_KEY_WORD: Int = 0
public static WAIT_TAG_WORD: Int = 1
public static WAIT_PAYLOAD_WORD: Int = 2
public static WAIT_BODY_LEN_WORD: Int = 3

/// **THE BUFFER A `Waiter.Wait` CALLER MUST SUPPLY** — the header, then a body
/// region big enough for the largest delivery there is (sawos design 15).
///
/// **IT IS MAX-SIZED AND THE COPY IS NOT** (ruled Sep 1, user). A parked waiter
/// cannot predict which arm fires, so the BUFFER has to hold the biggest answer
/// any of its attachments could give; the KERNEL copies only what the delivery
/// needs — four header words for a word-payload kind, four words plus
/// `body_len` for a reply carrying data. So the record's growth costs a
/// non-pipe waiter buffer SPACE and never copy TIME.
///
/// A STRUCT RATHER THAN A WORD ARRAY, and the reason is the body's alignment.
/// Declaration order IS the layout (design 58), so the header words put the
/// struct at machine-word alignment — which `Waiter.Wait` requires — and the
/// body region lands at exactly `wait_header_bytes()`, which is the offset the
/// kernel writes it at. A `[UInt8; N]` beside a `[UInt; N]` in one array could
/// not have said that.
public struct WaitBuffer {
    public header: [UInt; WAIT_HEADER_WORDS],
    public body: [UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
}

/// Bytes in a wait record's HEADER — where the body region starts, and exactly
/// what a word-payload delivery writes.
///
/// A FUNCTION AND NOT A `static`, which is a language limitation rather than a
/// choice: `sizeof<UInt>()` does not fold in a static initializer, even though
/// design 186's own diagnostic lists it among the things that do (DF-178f). The
/// arithmetic folds perfectly well in an ordinary expression, so the constant is
/// spelled where it is used and this is the one place it is written.
public func wait_header_bytes() -> Int {
    WAIT_HEADER_WORDS * sizeof<UInt>()
}

/// Bytes in a wait record BUFFER on THIS machine — the minimum capacity a caller
/// must give `Waiter.Wait`, and what the kernel checks the buffer against.
///
/// **IT MOVED IN M4 UNIT 3, AND THE vDSO DISCIPLINE IS WHAT MADE THAT FREE**
/// (design 10 ruling 11(a)): every caller's buffer comes out of `sos.waiter`'s
/// own frame, so the number is written once here and read once there.
public func wait_record_bytes() -> Int {
    sizeof<WaitBuffer>()
}

static_assert(sizeof<WaitBuffer>() >= WAIT_HEADER_WORDS * sizeof<UInt>()
                                      + PIPE_BODY_BYTES,
              "a wait buffer must hold the header AND a whole message body")

/// What became ready, decoded — one case per waitable kind.
///
/// The Event's payload is its ACCUMULATED WORD, and carrying it here is what
/// makes the common wait one syscall instead of two: a dispatcher that only
/// needs to know how many times something happened never has to follow up with
/// a `receive`.
///
/// **THE DELIVERY CONSUMES THE WORD** (ruled Aug 17, user). This record is not a
/// copy left beside the object — the kernel reads the word and clears it in one
/// masked-interrupts step, so the bits in the record are bits nothing else will
/// ever be told about. A `receive` right after a wake therefore answers ZERO
/// unless somebody signalled in between, and a signal that lands after the take
/// re-arms readiness with its own bits alone.
///
/// TWO DOORS ONTO ONE VALUE, and that is the whole model: §2.4's `receive` is
/// the non-blocking poll and this record is the blocking one. A process picks
/// the door by whether it wants to park, never by what it will be told. The
/// alternative — a snapshot the wait leaves behind — would report the same bits
/// to the next wait as well, and a recipient cannot tell that duplicate from a
/// real second signal.
/// THE INTERRUPT'S PAYLOAD IS ITS LINE, and that is the whole answer a driver
/// needs: it woke because a device asserted, and the line says which. There is
/// no count and no timestamp, because §9 ratifies AT MOST ONE UNACKED FIRE per
/// Interrupt — a second assertion while the first is unacked cannot happen, the
/// line being masked — so "how many" is always one and would be a field that
/// only ever held the same number.
///
/// A driver that owns exactly one line already knows which it is, so the field
/// looks redundant there; it is not, because the shape §2.2 exists for is a
/// dispatcher parked on SEVERAL. Such a dispatcher gets the line without a
/// side-table, exactly as it gets the key.
/// THE TIMER'S PAYLOAD IS ITS FIRE COUNT, and it is a count where the
/// Interrupt's deliberately is not (design 232 unit 1). The two differ because
/// their invariants do: §9 masks a line on fire, so an Interrupt can never have
/// two outstanding and "how many" would always read one — while a Timer has no
/// mask and nothing to hold it back, so a periodic one whose consumer was
/// descheduled genuinely expired several times. COALESCING those into a number
/// is what stops a slow consumer from either falling permanently behind a queue
/// of fires or silently losing them.
///
/// It SATURATES rather than wrapping, on §2.4's counting-Event reasoning
/// exactly: zero is what "not ready" means, so a count that rolled through it
/// would make a busy timer go quiet.
///
/// THE WAIT CONSUMES IT, exactly as it consumes an Event's word: the count is
/// drained by the wait that reports it, because a Timer has no second op to
/// drain with and needs none — the fire was delivered to somebody, so it is
/// spent. That is the ack-free half of the timerfd model. The Interrupt is the
/// kind that differs, and §9 says why: its readiness ends at the driver's `ack`,
/// because the line stays masked until the DEVICE has been serviced.
///
/// **THE PROCESS PAYLOAD IS §8's STATUS WORD, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT
/// UNIT 5.5 ADDED TO THIS ENUM** (sawos design 8). Kind in the high bits,
/// code in the low ones — read it with the same arithmetic `get_status`
/// answers with, because it IS that answer: attaching a Process handle and
/// polling one are two doors onto one word, exactly as `receive` and a wait
/// are two doors onto an Event's.
///
/// **NOTHING UN-DIES, so the wait does NOT consume it.** An Event's word and a
/// Timer's fire count are spent by the delivery that reports them; a death is
/// the TERMINAL LEVEL, so the second wait on one attachment answers the same
/// word as the first, and a waiter that attaches after the death is told at
/// once. That is the property supervision needs — there is no window in which a
/// death can be missed — and it is why this case has nothing to say about
/// draining.
///
/// **THE FOUR PIPE ARMS ARE M4 UNIT 3's** (sawos design 15), and one of them is
/// unlike everything above it: `Reply` CARRIES THE ANSWER'S BYTES. The other
/// three are word-shaped levels in the mould of the four that shipped — a
/// readable outlet, a postable inlet, an abandoned claim — and the reply is
/// design 10 ruling 11(a)'s whole point, which is that the wait IS the delivery
/// and a composed client never has to call `resolve` at all.
public enum WaitPayload {
    case Event(word: UInt),
    case Interrupt(line: UInt),
    case Timer(fires: UInt),
    case Process(status: UInt),
    /// The SERVER end has something, or will never have anything again.
    case Readable(state: OutletLevel),
    /// The CLIENT end has room to post, or nobody is left to take.
    case Room(state: InletLevel),
    /// The answer to one exchange — the bytes, or the news that nobody will
    /// ever write them.
    case Reply(outcome: ReplyDelivery),
    /// The claim behind this obligation was dropped. There is nothing to say
    /// beyond the fact, which is why this case has no payload.
    case Abandoned,
}

/// The tier the `Reply` payload carries upward (design 139's wrapper rule): an
/// enum holding a NoCopy payload declares a policy exactly as a struct with such
/// a field does, and this is the compiler asking for it.
extension WaitPayload: NoCopy {}

/// A `PipeOutlet`'s readable level, decoded (sawos design 15).
///
/// **DRAIN-THEN-TERMINAL IS THE ORDER, so `Msg` WINS while anything is staged**
/// (design 230's close-drains-first rule, which `PipeOutletOp.Take` already
/// follows): a sender exiting mid-conversation does not un-send what it sent, so
/// a waiter is told `PeerGone` only once the ring is dry as well.
public enum OutletLevel: UInt {
    case Msg = 0,
    case PeerGone = 1,
}

/// A `PipeInlet`'s room-to-post level, decoded (design 10 ruling 8).
///
/// **`PeerGone` WINS HERE, and the asymmetry with `OutletLevel` is the ops'**:
/// `PipeInletOp.Post` asks about the outlet column BEFORE it asks about the
/// ring, because nobody being able to take is the stronger fact — a free slot
/// would be a place to put a message with no future. So a waiter learns which
/// of the two it woke for without a probe post, which is what ruling 8 asks the
/// payload word for.
public enum InletLevel: UInt {
    case Room = 0,
    case PeerGone = 1,
}

/// Which of `ReplyDelivery`'s two shapes a reply record carries — the wire
/// discriminator that its decoded form is built from.
public enum ReplyLevel: UInt {
    case Data = 0,
    case PeerClosed = 1,
}

/// A message body, and how many of its bytes are real (sawos design 15).
///
/// **ONE STRUCT, DECLARED BY THE UNIT THAT NEEDED IT FIRST** (user-ruled Sep 1,
/// over an inline array case): unit 4's message-carrying deliveries will hand
/// over exactly this shape beside their handles, so a reply delivery and a
/// message delivery grow ONE type rather than two that must be kept alike.
///
/// **`NoCopy`, AND THAT IS API DISCIPLINE RATHER THAN OWNERSHIP.** The bytes are
/// plain data and would copy for free (design 159's automatic tier), which is
/// exactly the problem: `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` of inline storage should not be
/// duplicated by an accident of binding. Callers that only route the bytes take
/// them by REFERENCE out of the payload.
public struct PipeMsg {
    public len: UInt,
    public bytes: [UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
}

extension PipeMsg: NoCopy {}

/// What arrived for a claim: the answer, or the news that there will not be one.
///
/// **`PeerClosed` IS THE SAME FACT `PipeReplyOp.Resolve` REPORTS** and it means
/// exactly what it means there — the server dropped its obligation, or the
/// connection's server end went while the message was still staged. There is no
/// third case, because a `Waiter` only ever delivers a level that is READY and
/// "still pending" is what not-ready means.
public enum ReplyDelivery {
    case Data(msg: PipeMsg),
    case PeerClosed,
}

extension ReplyDelivery: NoCopy {}

/// `Waiter.Wait`'s answer: the attacher's key, and what fired.
///
/// **`NoCopy` SINCE M4 UNIT 3** (sawos design 15), by the wrapper-carries-tier
/// rule and by API discipline together: the `Reply` payload owns
/// `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` of inline storage, so a result that duplicated on a plain
/// binding would copy a message body where nothing asked for one. Reading the
/// answer is a `match` on the PLACE — `match result.what`, which borrows — so
/// nothing about how the four shipped kinds are read changed.
public struct WaitResult {
    /// The word supplied at `add`, handed back unread.
    public key: UInt,
    public what: WaitPayload,
}

extension WaitResult: NoCopy {}

// =============================================================================
// The Clock and Timer records (spec §2's Clock/Timer rows; design 232 unit 1)
// =============================================================================
//
// TWO MORE RECORDS, AND ONE OF THEM GOES THE OTHER WAY. `Clock.Now` answers
// with a copy-OUT record on the `Waiter.Wait` precedent; `Timer.Arm` takes its
// arguments through a copy-IN record, which is the funnel's mirror twin and is
// built here because M4's Pipe `send` needs exactly this shape for a message
// body (§2.1). Timer.Arm is its small first consumer precisely as Waiter.Wait
// was for copy-out.
//
// WHY EITHER NEEDS A RECORD AT ALL: a NANOSECOND COUNT IS 64 BITS. Anything
// narrower wraps in four seconds, which is not a clock. One of the two profiles
// has 32-bit registers, so one time is two words and `arm`'s pair is four —
// past the three argument registers §5.7 gives an op, and past what a single
// value register can answer with.
//
// THESE RECORDS ARE INDEXED IN DOUBLEWORDS, NOT MACHINE WORDS, and that is the
// one way they differ from the wait record above. A wait record's fields are a
// KEY, a TAG and a PAYLOAD — all of them machine-word-shaped things (a task
// identity, a line number, an accumulated word), so it follows the register.
// These fields are TIMES, and a time is 64 bits on every machine SOS will ever
// run on, so following the register would make the layout differ per profile
// for no reason. Both still stay inside one address space, so design 47's
// wire-format discipline still does not apply — what applies is that the field
// width is a property of the QUANTITY rather than of the machine.

/// Doublewords in a `Clock.Now` answer: the reading, and nothing else.
public static CLOCK_NOW_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS: Int = 1

/// Where the reading sits. Named rather than written, on both sides.
public static CLOCK_NOW_TIME_SLOT: Int = 0

/// Bytes in a `Clock.Now` record — the minimum capacity a caller must give the
/// op, and what the kernel checks the buffer against.
///
/// A FUNCTION AND NOT A `static` for the reason `wait_record_bytes` gives:
/// `sizeof<T>()` does not fold in a static initializer (DF-178f) and folds
/// perfectly well in an ordinary expression.
public func clock_now_record_bytes() -> Int {
    CLOCK_NOW_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS * sizeof<UInt64>()
}

/// Doublewords in a `Timer.Arm` request: the first deadline, then the interval.
public static TIMER_ARM_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS: Int = 2

/// Where each lives.
///
/// `AFTER` IS RELATIVE AND `INTERVAL` IS NOT A TIME AT ALL, which is the one
/// thing a reader has to carry away from this layout. The first field says how
/// long from NOW the first fire is — relative, because an absolute first
/// deadline would make every caller read the clock first and race between the
/// two syscalls. The second is a PERIOD, and zero means one-shot: a timer that
/// fires once and disarms itself.
public static TIMER_ARM_AFTER_SLOT: Int = 0
public static TIMER_ARM_INTERVAL_SLOT: Int = 1

/// Bytes in a `Timer.Arm` record — exactly what the caller must hand over, and
/// what the kernel checks the length against.
public func timer_arm_record_bytes() -> Int {
    TIMER_ARM_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS * sizeof<UInt64>()
}

// =============================================================================
// The boot-handle record (spec §12; sawos design 2 D-3, settling sawlang#232
// agenda item 5)
// =============================================================================
//
// `Process.BootHandleNext` answers with a RECORD the kernel copies out, on the
// `Waiter.Wait` precedent exactly and through the same funnel. Three machine
// words: which region this is, what kind of object was minted for it, and the
// handle.
//
// IT IS MACHINE WORDS, NOT DOUBLEWORDS, and the rule is the wait record's: a
// tag, a kind and a handle are all machine-word-shaped things, so the layout
// follows the register. Only a TIME is a fixed 64 bits on every profile.
//
// THE RECORD IS CONSUMED BY DELIVERY, AND THERE ARE TWO DELIVERIES. The drain
// hands back the next undelivered record and advances; there is no rewind, no
// peek and no count op. A drained iterator answers `SosStatus.Drained` forever —
// a loop's termination check, never a fault, because exhaustion is exactly what
// a caller could not have known in advance and is the ordinary way the loop
// ends. **THE OTHER DELIVERY IS `start(boot_tag:)`'s RESOLUTION** (the Aug-29
// user ruling): putting a record's word in the child's first argument REGISTER
// is a delivery like any other, so that record is spent and the drain skips it.
// One record, one delivery, whichever door it went out of — which is what makes
// "a child can never be handed one word twice" a property of the kernel rather
// than an obligation on the child.
//
// **THE SET IS PER-PROCESS SINCE M3 UNIT 3** (sawos design 4 D-2), and there are
// now two ways one gets filled: the KERNEL writes root's at boot, one record per
// row of the build-emitted region table, and a LAUNCHER writes a child's with
// `ProcessOp.Give`, one record per capability it hands across. Nothing about the
// record or the op distinguishes them — the kernel is the giver nobody gave to.
// The set FREEZES at `Start` (a give afterwards is a `BadState` fault), which is
// what makes a half-populated table unrepresentable at the child's first
// instruction with no synchronization invented; the DRAIN has no deadline at
// all, so a child that drains late drains correctly.

/// Words in a boot-handle record: tag, kind, handle.
public static BOOT_HANDLE_RECORD_WORDS: Int = 3

/// Where each lives. Named rather than written, on both sides of the boundary.
public static BOOT_HANDLE_TAG_WORD: Int = 0
public static BOOT_HANDLE_KIND_WORD: Int = 1
public static BOOT_HANDLE_HANDLE_WORD: Int = 2

/// Bytes in a boot-handle record — the minimum capacity a caller must give the
/// op, and what the kernel checks the buffer against.
///
/// A FUNCTION AND NOT A `static` for the reason `wait_record_bytes` gives:
/// `sizeof<T>()` does not fold in a static initializer (DF-178f).
public func boot_handle_record_bytes() -> Int {
    BOOT_HANDLE_RECORD_WORDS * sizeof<UInt>()
}

/// What kind of object a boot-handle record names — the record's KIND WORD.
///
/// PUBLIC API rather than kernel bookkeeping, on the module docstring's line:
/// the kernel WRITES this value and userspace READS it, so one declaration has
/// to serve both. It is deliberately NOT `ObjType`, which is kernel-internal
/// and renumberable — a boot handle's kind crosses the trap boundary, so it is
/// its own closed set with its own stated numbers.
///
/// M3 unit 2 declared one kind, because the boot set was exactly the region
/// table's rows. **UNIT 3 DECLARES THE SECOND** (sawos design 4 D-3): a give may
/// carry a PROCESS handle, and the one that matters is the child's own — the
/// drain lives on the Process object, so a child that is to drain must be handed
/// the handle `process_create` minted. The raw backing is what lets a kind be
/// ADDED without renumbering (design 145's wire idiom), which is exactly what
/// happened here and happened AGAIN at unit 4's `IoMemory` below; unit 5.5's
/// death channels are the next to want it.
///
/// **THE SET IS EXACTLY THE GIVABLE KINDS, and it is smaller than `ObjType` on
/// purpose.** A kind reaches this enum when something can actually deliver one:
/// v1 mints the universal `Transfer` bit in `memory_rights()`, in
/// `child_process_rights()` (D-3) and — since the Aug-29 rulings — in
/// `root_system_rights()`, and NOWHERE ELSE. So a Thread, Event, Waiter,
/// Interrupt, Clock or Timer handle cannot be given at all and a case for one
/// would be dead surface — the same rule `ClockType` follows in declaring one
/// domain. The kernel's `boot_kind_of` is the one place the two enums meet, and
/// it refuses what it cannot spell rather than inventing a number.
///
/// `System` is the one a launch actually uses: it is what a child receives in
/// its first argument register, a masked sibling of root's own, which is what
/// makes every process bootstrap the same way (§12).
///
/// Backed by `UInt`, the width of the word it travels in, for the reason
/// `WaitTag` gives: userspace decodes it through `from(raw:)`, which is total.
/// **THE TAG BESIDE IT IS THE GIVER'S OWN WORD, HANDED BACK UNREAD.** For root's
/// boot set the kernel is the giver and the tags are region ordinals (row `n` is
/// tag `n`, sawos design 2 D-3); for a child's set the LAUNCHER is the giver and
/// the tag is whatever it chose. The kernel knows nothing about either — which
/// ordinal is an image blob, which is destination RAM, which capability a child
/// expects under 7 — because THE TAG IS THE IDENTITY and identities are the only
/// cross-process vocabulary there is (design 4's ruled surface). Keeping the
/// meaning in root's config is what makes the kernel's half of the launch flow
/// the same code for a hand-built M3 system and for M4's dynamic loading.
/// **UNIT 4 DECLARES THE FOURTH** (sawos design 6 D-1/D-5), which is the
/// raw backing doing exactly what the paragraph above predicted: the region
/// table now carries a KIND column, a Device row mints an `IoMemory`, and root
/// receives it through the same drain as everything else. It is `Transfer`-able
/// (`iomemory_rights()`) because handing a driver child its window is unit 6's
/// whole flow. **THAT FLOW IS BUILT** (sawos design 9 D-2): root drains the
/// console's window at boot and GIVES it to a child that maps it into itself and
/// echoes the harness's bytes, on both profiles — so this case is now exercised
/// end to end rather than merely declared.
///
/// There is deliberately NO `Mapping` case. A Mapping is not givable —
/// `mapping_rights()` withholds `Transfer` on purpose (see `MappingRight`) — so
/// a case for one would be dead surface on the rule this set already follows.
public enum BootHandleKind: UInt {
    case Memory = 0,
    case Process = 1,
    case System = 2,
    case IoMemory = 3,
    /// **M4 UNIT 1's PAIR, AND THE RAW BACKING DOING WHAT IT KEEPS DOING**
    /// (sawos design 13 D-3): two more givable kinds took the next two numbers
    /// and moved none of the four above them. `pipe_inlet_rights()` and
    /// `pipe_outlet_rights()` mint `Transfer` because handing a child ONE END of
    /// a connection is the whole point of the pair — a launcher creates it and
    /// wires the two ends outward, which is what makes `pipe-child` the seed of
    /// unit 5's driver-as-service.
    case PipeInlet = 4,
    case PipeOutlet = 5,
    /// **M4 UNIT 2's ONE-SHOTS, AND THIS PAIR IS THE ONE §2.1 ARGUED FOR BY
    /// NAME** (sawos design 14). `pipe_reply_rights()` and
    /// `pipe_request_rights()` mint `Transfer` because the ratified section says
    /// both one-shots are transferable and calls it THE DELEGATION PRIMITIVE: a
    /// server MOVES its obligation to a third party, who replies straight to the
    /// original client, and the payload never transits the middle process. So a
    /// give must be able to carry one, and a give's record must be able to spell
    /// it.
    case PipeReply = 6,
    case PipeRequest = 7,
}

// THE DECODED RECORD IS NOT DECLARED HERE, and the asymmetry with `WaitResult`
// is worth one note. A wait answer is made of plain words — a key, a tag, a
// count — so one declaration serves the kernel that writes it and the process
// that reads it. A boot-handle answer carries a HANDLE, and the whole point of
// the `sos` module's typed layer is that a handle a process holds is a `Memory`
// or a `Process` and never a word. So the decoded form is `sos`'s
// `BootHandle`, which this file could not name; what crosses is the three-word
// layout above, and that is declared once, here.

// =============================================================================
// The pipe records (spec §2.1; sawos designs 13 and 14)
// =============================================================================
//
// TWO PUBLISHED NUMBERS AND ONE RECORD, and they are here rather than in
// `kcore.limits` for the reason `wait_record_bytes()` is here: THEY ARE THE
// CONTRACT BETWEEN THE TWO HALVES. Design 10 ruling 6 asks for the limits as
// named build defines and does not say which module — `kcore.limits` is where a
// SLAB dimension belongs, and a slab dimension is a fact about the kernel that
// userspace never needs. `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` is the other kind: a `Post` caller
// must not name more and a `Take` caller must supply at least that much, so it
// is exactly the published minimum a copy funnel's two sides have to agree on.
// One declaration, in the module both halves compile against.
//
// `PIPE_INFLIGHT` STAYED IN `kcore.limits` on the same cut, and it is the honest
// half of it: how deep the staging ring is derives from `MAX_THREADS`, and a
// caller that meets a full ring is told `WouldBlock` without ever having to know
// the number.

// **`PIPE_BODY_BYTES` USED TO BE DECLARED HERE**, and it moved to just above the
// wait record (sawos design 15, M4 unit 3). Nothing about it changed except its
// line: ruling 11(a) put a BODY REGION of exactly that many bytes into every
// wait-record buffer, an array length is a constant expression, and statics fold
// in DECLARATION ORDER — so the number has to be above the first thing that
// reads it. The reasoning for why it is in `sosabi` at all is unchanged and is
// carried with it.

/// Words in a `Process.PipeCreate` answer: the inlet handle, then the outlet.
///
/// **MACHINE WORDS, NOT DOUBLEWORDS**, and the rule is the boot-handle record's:
/// a HANDLE is a machine-word-shaped thing, so the layout follows the register.
/// Only a TIME is a fixed 64 bits on every profile.
public static PIPE_CREATE_RECORD_WORDS: Int = 2

/// Where each end's handle lives. Named rather than written, on both sides of
/// the boundary. The ORDER is inlet-then-outlet — client end first, the way the
/// pair reads in every sentence about it.
public static PIPE_CREATE_INLET_WORD: Int = 0
public static PIPE_CREATE_OUTLET_WORD: Int = 1

/// Bytes in a `Process.PipeCreate` record — the minimum capacity a caller must
/// give the op, and what the kernel checks the buffer against.
///
/// A FUNCTION AND NOT A `static` for the reason `wait_record_bytes` gives:
/// `sizeof<T>()` does not fold in a static initializer (DF-178f).
public func pipe_create_record_bytes() -> Int {
    PIPE_CREATE_RECORD_WORDS * sizeof<UInt>()
}

/// Words in a `PipeOutlet.Take` answer: the message's LENGTH, then the
/// `PipeRequest` handle that obliges its reply (spec §2.1; sawos design 14).
///
/// **A TAKE ANSWERS TWO THINGS NOW, SO IT ANSWERS IN MEMORY.** Unit 1's take put
/// the length in the value register because that was all it had to say; unit 2
/// mints the obligation at the take, and one op returns one word. The record is
/// the door `ProcessOp.PipeCreate` already opened for exactly this reason, and
/// the BODY stays a separate buffer because it is a separate thing — bytes the
/// caller sized against `PIPE_BODY_BYTES`, not a word the kernel assembled.
public static PIPE_TAKE_RECORD_WORDS: Int = 2

/// Where each half of a take's answer lives. LENGTH first, because the message
/// is what a take is FOR and the obligation is what comes with it.
public static PIPE_TAKE_LEN_WORD: Int = 0
public static PIPE_TAKE_REQUEST_WORD: Int = 1

/// Bytes in a `PipeOutlet.Take` record — the minimum capacity a caller must give
/// the op. A FUNCTION for `pipe_create_record_bytes`' reason (DF-178f).
public func pipe_take_record_bytes() -> Int {
    PIPE_TAKE_RECORD_WORDS * sizeof<UInt>()
}

/// Words in a `PipeRequest.ReplyWait` ARGUMENT record: the waiter handle, the
/// reply body's address, the reply body's length (sawos design 17).
///
/// **IT GOES THE OTHER WAY — THIS IS A COPY-IN RECORD**, `TimerOp.Arm`'s door
/// rather than `PipeOutletOp.Take`'s, and it exists for the plainest reason
/// §5.7 gives: the op has FOUR things to say and three argument registers. A
/// reply body, its length, a waiter and a destination for the delivery do not
/// fit, so the three SMALL words ride the funnel and the register that is left
/// carries the wait buffer.
///
/// **THE BODY STAYS AN ADDRESS RATHER THAN JOINING THE RECORD**, which is the
/// one design decision in the layout: a record holding the body itself would
/// make every `reply_wait` copy `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` in userspace before the trap,
/// where an address costs one word and the kernel's own copy-in is the only
/// copy there is. The address is checked by the copy door like any other.
public static PIPE_REPLY_WAIT_RECORD_WORDS: Int = 3

/// Where each argument lives. Named rather than written, on both sides of the
/// boundary. The WAITER comes first because it is what makes this op different
/// from the `Reply` it is otherwise a copy of.
public static PIPE_REPLY_WAIT_WAITER_WORD: Int = 0
public static PIPE_REPLY_WAIT_BODY_WORD: Int = 1
public static PIPE_REPLY_WAIT_LEN_WORD: Int = 2

/// Bytes in a `PipeRequest.ReplyWait` argument record — EXACTLY what the op
/// must be handed. A FUNCTION for `pipe_create_record_bytes`' reason (DF-178f).
public func pipe_reply_wait_record_bytes() -> Int {
    PIPE_REPLY_WAIT_RECORD_WORDS * sizeof<UInt>()
}

// =============================================================================
// The process-stats record (spec §2's Process row; sawos design 16)
// =============================================================================
//
// **DOUBLEWORDS, NOT MACHINE WORDS, AND THE CLOCK RECORD'S REASON IS THIS
// RECORD'S REASON**: a COUNT is a property of the quantity, not of the machine.
// A 32-bit column on riscv32 would wrap after four billion traps and a 64-bit
// one on arm64 would not, so the same program would answer two different things
// about itself depending on which profile it was built for. Sixty-four bits on
// both, exactly as a nanosecond count is.
//
// **WHAT EACH COLUMN COUNTS, AND WHICH ONE A TEST MAY ASSERT.** The split is by
// the CAUSE CLASS the trap entry decides on (`ktrap`'s own three-way order),
// which is why it is the split that makes the numbers useful:
//
//   `syscalls`    every trap the process took through the syscall instruction —
//                 one per `ecall`/`svc`, whatever the op, whatever the answer,
//                 and ONE for a call that parked for a millisecond. So it is
//                 EXACT for a given program path and it is the column an
//                 assertion should read.
//   `interrupts`  every timer or device interrupt taken while this process was
//                 the current one. NONDETERMINISTIC by construction — the tick
//                 lands where it lands — so it is printed and never asserted,
//                 the timing-rows doctrine at a smaller scale.
//   `faults`      every other exception: an access fault, a misaligned access,
//                 an illegal instruction. Exact where a test provokes one on
//                 purpose, and normally zero, because a fault ends the process
//                 (§8) — so the interesting reader is a SUPERVISOR holding the
//                 dead child's handle, not the process itself.
//
// **A BAD SYSCALL COUNTS AS A SYSCALL AND NOT AS A FAULT.** A word naming no
// handle, an op the object does not have, a right that was not given: each of
// those ENDS the process (design 178's faults ruling), but the trap that carried
// it arrived through the syscall instruction, and this record's columns are keyed
// by how the machine entered the kernel rather than by how the kernel felt about
// it. Keying on the outcome instead would make `syscalls` un-assertable, because
// a caller cannot know in advance which of its calls the kernel will refuse.
//
// **ATTRIBUTION IS TO THE PROCESS CURRENT AT TRAP ENTRY**, decided before
// anything is dispatched, so a syscall that switches processes is charged to the
// one that made it and an interrupt that preempts is charged to the one that was
// running. Traps taken through the BOOT DOOR, before any process exists, are
// UNATTRIBUTED — the harness kernels that enter user mode with no object model
// have a running program and no process slot, and there is nowhere honest to put
// their count. `ktrap` says so at the site.
//
// **THE COLUMNS ZERO AT PROCESS CREATE**, both at root's boot-time reification
// and at `SystemOp.ProcessCreate`, so a reused slot never inherits its
// predecessor's traffic.

/// Doublewords in a `Process.Stats` answer: syscalls, interrupts, faults.
public static PROCESS_STATS_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS: Int = 3

/// Where each column sits. Named rather than written, on both sides of the
/// boundary. The ORDER is the order the paragraph above reads them in, which is
/// also assertable-first.
public static PROCESS_STATS_SYSCALLS_SLOT: Int = 0
public static PROCESS_STATS_INTERRUPTS_SLOT: Int = 1
public static PROCESS_STATS_FAULTS_SLOT: Int = 2

/// Bytes in a `Process.Stats` record — the minimum capacity a caller must give
/// the op, and what the kernel checks the buffer against.
///
/// A FUNCTION AND NOT A `static` for the reason `wait_record_bytes` gives:
/// `sizeof<T>()` does not fold in a static initializer (DF-178f).
public func process_stats_record_bytes() -> Int {
    PROCESS_STATS_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS * sizeof<UInt64>()
}

// =============================================================================
// Status words (spec §5.7)
// =============================================================================

/// The status a syscall returns in the status register.
///
/// **A STATUS IS FOR WHAT THE CALLER COULD NOT HAVE KNOWN** (ratified Aug 8,
/// carried into design 178's ruling Aug 15). A caller error the process could
/// have checked — an invalid handle, an op the object does not have, a right it
/// was not given, driving a thread that is not itself — TERMINATES the process
/// and is not in this enum at all. It is a `FaultReason` below. What is left
/// here is the class a correct process can still meet: resource exhaustion, and
/// (when there are peers to die, which is M3) peer death.
///
/// That cut is why `BadHandle`, `BadOp` and `AccessDenied` are GONE from this
/// enum rather than merely unused. They moved to `FaultReason` keeping their
/// numbers, so the tags read the same in a kernel report and cannot be confused
/// with a status a process might branch on. Values 1-3 are left as a gap here
/// for exactly that reason: reusing them would make an old build's `BadHandle`
/// and a new build's status collide on the wire.
///
/// These tags ARE part of the contract in a way the op numbers are not, because
/// a process branches on them, so the backing is FIXED-WIDTH (design 47: a
/// value that crosses an ABI pins its width) even though the register carrying
/// it is wider.
///
/// A STATUS, NOT AN ERROR (ratified Aug 7): the type is named for what it is —
/// an enum with an `Ok` case describes how a call went, and only some of those
/// ways are failures. The `Sos` prefix says whose status it is, which is the
/// other half of the rename: the hosted runtime has its own frozen `SysError`
/// (errno tags, `sawc/rt/ABI.md`) and the two contracts are unrelated. They now
/// read as unrelated too.
///
/// `Ok` is a case because this enum is the wire table and 0 is in it. It is the
/// one value that is not a failure, and `sos`'s `checked` is the single place
/// that turns it into `Ok(())` — which is why an `Err(SosStatus.Ok)` cannot be
/// constructed anywhere downstream.
///
/// `Unknown` is NOT a status the kernel returns. It is where the userspace
/// mapping's `from(raw:)` miss lands, so a tag this build does not define stays
/// a typed error instead of being read as success. In M1 it is unreachable —
/// both halves compile from this one declaration — and it exists for the build
/// skew that static linking makes impossible today and a mapped vDSO will not.
/// **THE TWO M3-UNIT-2 CASES, and each is here because of the same cut** (sawos
/// design 2 D-3/D-4; the numbers continue past the 1-3 gap rather than filling
/// it, for the reason above).
///
/// `BadImage` (5) is a malformed process image handed to `process_create`.
/// sawlang#232 agenda item 2 leaned this way and design 2 ruled it: IMAGE BYTES
/// ARE DATA, on the `from(raw:)` precedent — an unrecognized wire byte is data
/// a reader rejects, never a trap — and a launcher that fetches images
/// dynamically (M4) must be able to report a bad download rather than die of
/// it. It is the one place the faults ruling's line falls on the STATUS side
/// for something the caller supplied, and the reason it does is that the caller
/// did not WRITE those bytes: it was handed a region and asked to launch it.
///
/// `Drained` (6) is the boot-handle iterator having nothing left to give. It is
/// a status and not a fault for the plainest possible reason: a loop that
/// drains an iterator ENDS by meeting it, so faulting would make the correct
/// program the one that dies. It STAYS drained — every later call answers the
/// same — so a caller may test it as often as it likes.
///
/// **`QuotaExceeded` (7) IS POLICY'S VOICE, AND `NoResource` IS THE MACHINE'S**
/// (sawos design 7 D-3). They are two different answers to what used to be one
/// refusal, and a caller can act on the difference: `NoResource` says the SLAB
/// is full — a machine-wide edge, nothing this process can do about it —
/// while `QuotaExceeded` says THIS PROCESS has spent its budget, which a
/// release of something it already holds will fix. A launcher meeting the
/// first has met the end of the machine; a program meeting the second has met
/// the end of its allowance, and the two want different code.
///
/// IT IS A STATUS AND NOT A FAULT, which the sharpened faults line decides on
/// its own terms: quota headroom is DYNAMIC resource vocabulary — how much of
/// its budget a process has left changes as objects it does not own are freed
/// and as its own peers release — so it is precisely the thing a caller could
/// not have checked. That is the same carve-out `NoResource` sits in, which is
/// why the two are neighbours here rather than one being a `FaultReason`.
///
/// **`Revoked` (8) IS THE PEER-GONE DOCTRINE'S ONE WORD** (sawos design 12, M4
/// unit 0; `designs/010` rulings 1 and 2). It answers a call that was PARKED on
/// something which can no longer happen: today the one site is a `Waiter.Wait`
/// whose Waiter reached zero references while the caller was on its blocked
/// list, and the doctrine it lands is that nothing parked is ever silently
/// doomed — every "what you are waiting for is gone" is a DELIVERED WAKE
/// carrying a distinguishable status, never a strand for the deadlock report to
/// name later.
///
/// IT IS A STATUS AND NOT A FAULT, and the cut is the faults ruling's own. The
/// mistake belongs to whoever released the handle, and this word is delivered to
/// somebody ELSE — a sibling thread that made no error and cannot be terminated
/// for one it did not make. A parked caller is also in the one position from
/// which nothing could have been checked in advance: the answer changed after
/// the call was made.
///
/// **IT IS ONE WORD FOR EVERY REVOCATION SITE, deliberately** (ruling 1's own
/// text), which is why it is not named for the Waiter. M4's later rungs reach
/// the same doctrine with their own vocabulary — a pipe's peer closing is
/// `PeerClosed`, because "the far end went away" is information a protocol acts
/// on — and what belongs here is the case where the OBJECT BEING WAITED ON
/// stopped existing under the waiter. A caller distinguishes the two, which is
/// the whole reason neither is folded into the other.
/// **`PeerClosed` (9) IS THE PEER-GONE DOCTRINE'S OTHER WORD** (sawos design 13,
/// M4 unit 1; `designs/010` D-1 and ruling 2). It is §2.1's own term, and it
/// answers a call whose FAR END no longer exists: a `Post` through an inlet
/// whose outlet column reached zero (nobody can ever take it), and a `Take`
/// through an outlet whose inlet column reached zero AND whose ring has been
/// drained (no sender can ever appear, since attenuation is monotonic and the
/// count is zero).
///
/// **IT IS NOT `Revoked`, AND THE TWO MUST NOT FOLD INTO EACH OTHER.** `Revoked`
/// is for the OBJECT BEING WAITED ON ceasing to exist under a parked caller —
/// nothing to act on, only to report. `PeerClosed` is INFORMATION A PROTOCOL
/// ACTS ON: the connection is still there and still yours, and what changed is
/// that the other role is unoccupied. A client meeting it stops sending; a
/// server meeting it closes the session. `Revoked`'s own docstring names this
/// split from the other side.
///
/// **IT IS TERMINAL.** Nothing un-closes — the ends' reference columns only ever
/// fall to zero and a zero column can never rise (a handle onto an end is what
/// would raise it, and there is none) — so a caller may test it as often as it
/// likes and will be told the same. That is design 8's terminal-level shape
/// arriving in a second vocabulary.
///
/// **`WouldBlock` (10) IS ONE TRANSIENT FOR BOTH DIRECTIONS**: a `Post` whose
/// ring is full, and a `Take` whose ring is empty. It says CALL AGAIN — the
/// answer will change when the peer acts — which is the exact opposite of the
/// case above and is why the two could not share a word.
///
/// ONE CASE AND NOT TWO, deliberately, and the reason is design 234's
/// narrowest-type doctrine read the way that doctrine actually runs: a status is
/// narrow when it names ONE CONDITION, and post-full and take-empty are the same
/// condition — the ring is not in a state to serve this call yet. Which
/// direction it was is already in the op the caller made, so a `PostWouldBlock`
/// beside a `TakeWouldBlock` would encode the receiver twice and force every
/// poll loop to match two tags that mean one thing. `describe()` is worded for
/// the CONDITION rather than for either op, which is the same rule stated in the
/// text a human reads.
///
/// IT IS A STATUS AND NOT A FAULT, and the cut is the faults ruling's own: how
/// full a shared ring is changes as the PEER acts, so it is precisely the thing
/// a caller could not have checked in advance. It is `NoResource`'s neighbour in
/// spirit — a resource edge — and differs in being the peer's to clear rather
/// than the machine's.
public enum SosStatus: UInt8 {
    case Ok = 0,
    case NoResource = 4,
    case BadImage = 5,
    case Drained = 6,
    case QuotaExceeded = 7,
    case Revoked = 8,
    case PeerClosed = 9,
    case WouldBlock = 10,
    case Unknown = 255,
}

extension SosStatus {
    /// A short human-readable tag, for a diagnostic or a log line.
    public func describe(&self) -> String {
        match self {
            case Ok -> "ok",
            case NoResource -> "out of kernel objects",
            case BadImage -> "not a loadable process image",
            case Drained -> "no more boot handles",
            case QuotaExceeded -> "this process is at its quota",
            case Revoked -> "what this call was waiting for is gone",
            case PeerClosed -> "the other end of this connection is gone",
            // WORDED FOR THE CONDITION, NOT FOR THE OP (the case's own
            // docstring): one sentence has to be true of a full ring and of an
            // empty one, because one tag answers both.
            case WouldBlock -> "not right now — try again",
            case Unknown -> "unknown status",
        }
    }

    /// Whether this status reports a failure. The inverse of `Ok`, named so a
    /// caller reads intent rather than a comparison.
    public func is_failure(&self) -> Bool {
        match self {
            case Ok -> false,
            case _ -> true,
        }
    }
}

extension SosStatus: Printable {
    public func format(&self, into: &var StringBuilder) {
        // design 234 §5's rendering boundary: `format` has no error channel,
        // and on SOS the builder is fixed storage anyway, which refuses nothing.
        try! into.append(self.describe())
    }
}

extension SosStatus: Error {}

// =============================================================================
// Fault reasons (design 178's faults ruling, ratified Aug 15)
// =============================================================================

/// Why the kernel TERMINATED a process.
///
/// The other half of the cut `SosStatus` describes: these are the caller errors
/// a process could have checked, and meeting one ends the process rather than
/// returning it a word to branch on. It is the same line the language draws at
/// an indexed accessor — an out-of-range index panics, it does not hand back a
/// flag — and it cuts userspace error handling down to the failures that are
/// real.
///
/// A process never RECEIVES one of these: it is dead by the time the tag
/// exists. It reaches a human through the kernel's report, and the harness
/// asserts on that line. The first three keep the numbers they had as statuses
/// so a reader of an old transcript and a new one sees the same tag for the
/// same mistake.
///
/// `UInt8` because it is a kernel-internal tag that crosses no register.
public enum FaultReason: UInt8 {
    case BadHandle = 1,
    case BadOp = 2,
    case AccessDenied = 3,
    case NotSelf = 4,
    case BadState = 5,
    case BadEntry = 6,
    case Deadlock = 7,
    case NotWaitable = 8,
    case BadArg = 9,
    case BadAlign = 10,
    case BadBuffer = 11,
    case DuplicateKey = 12,
    case LineBound = 13,
}

extension FaultReason {
    /// The short tag the kernel's termination line carries.
    public func describe(&self) -> String {
        match self {
            case BadHandle -> "bad handle",
            case BadOp -> "bad op",
            case AccessDenied -> "access denied",
            case NotSelf -> "not the calling thread",
            // Not "thread in the wrong state" any more: the tag has covered
            // every object kind since Events and Waiters landed, and it covers
            // an Interrupt acked before it fired now.
            case BadState -> "object in the wrong state",
            case BadEntry -> "thread entry outside the process",
            case Deadlock -> "every thread blocked",
            case NotWaitable -> "not a waitable",
            case BadArg -> "argument outside its domain",
            case BadAlign -> "buffer is not word-aligned",
            // Direction-neutral since design 232 unit 1 opened the copy-IN
            // door beside the copy-out one. The two check DIFFERENT windows —
            // outbound must land in the process's writable memory, inbound
            // merely inside the memory it was granted — so a wording naming
            // one of them was accurate for exactly as long as there was one.
            case BadBuffer -> "buffer is outside the process's memory",
            // TWO CONSUMERS SINCE M3 UNIT 3, and the WORDING DID NOT MOVE: a
            // give whose tag is already in the child's boot set raises this
            // exact tag (sawos design 4 — an identity naming two handles is
            // broken config, caller-checkable, and it would make the record a
            // multimap and the boot lookup ambiguous). The sentence names the
            // Waiter case because that is the one it was written for and
            // because the string is ASSERTED by a shipped transcript
            // (`event_dupkey`); design 4 authorised no expectation changes, so
            // the generalisation is recorded here rather than spelled in the
            // line. A later unit that touches that row should widen it to name
            // the key rather than the attachment.
            case DuplicateKey -> "an attachment already uses that key",
            case LineBound -> "that interrupt line is already bound",
        }
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// Process status (spec §8)
// =============================================================================

/// How a process ended, or that it has not. Spec §8: ONE fixed-width status
/// word, KIND in the high bits and a code in the low bits, so a supervisor
/// reads both out of one register.
///
/// `Killed` is declared and unreachable in M2 for the reason `ProcessRight`
/// gives: there is no second process to be killed by. It is here because the
/// word's ENCODING is what a supervisor parses, and a kind added later would
/// renumber nothing but would still be a wire change.
public enum ProcessStatusKind: UInt8 {
    case Running = 0,
    case Exited = 1,
    case Faulted = 2,
    case Killed = 3,
}

/// Where the kind sits in the status word. The low half is the code — an exit
/// status, or a `FaultReason` — so both fit a 32-bit reader on either profile.
static PROCESS_STATUS_KIND_SHIFT: Int = 16
static PROCESS_STATUS_CODE_MASK: UInt = 0xFFFF

/// Assemble the §8 status word.
public func process_status_word(kind: ProcessStatusKind, code: UInt) -> UInt {
    ((((kind as UInt8) as UInt) << PROCESS_STATUS_KIND_SHIFT)
     | (code & PROCESS_STATUS_CODE_MASK))
}

// =============================================================================
// The boot handle set (spec §12)
// =============================================================================

/// The handle the kernel gives root for the System object. Root receives it in
/// the first argument register at entry rather than assuming it, so this
/// constant is the KERNEL's choice of what to mint — not a number userspace is
/// expected to know.
public static ROOT_SYSTEM_HANDLE: UInt = 1

/// The `stack_top` handed to a user entry that was granted no stack region.
public static NO_USER_STACK: UInt = 0
